(function(){(window.PhysicaNeetYear=window.PhysicaNeetYear||{})[2018]=[{id:"neet-2018-q001",pn:1,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Direction of E, B and propagation",q:"An em wave is propagating in a medium with a velocity V\u20D7 = V \xEE. The instantaneous oscillating electric field of this em wave is along +y axis. Then the direction of oscillating magnetic field of the em wave will be along",o:["\u2013z direction","+z direction","\u2013y direction","\u2013x direction"],c:1,s:`In an em wave, E\u20D7 \xD7 B\u20D7 points along the direction of propagation.
Propagation is along +\xEE and E\u20D7 is along +\u0135.
So \xEE = \u0135 \xD7 B\u0302.
Since \u0135 \xD7 k\u0302 = \xEE, B\u0302 = +k\u0302.
The magnetic field is along +z.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Waves"},{id:"neet-2018-q002",pn:2,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Prism with a silvered face",q:"The refractive index of the material of a prism is \u221A2 and the angle of the prism is 30\xBA. One of the two refracting surfaces of the prism is made a mirror inwards, by silver coating. A beam of monochromatic light entering the prism from the other face will retrace its path (after reflection from the silvered surface) if its angle of incidence on the prism is -",o:["60\xBA","45\xBA","30\xBA","zero"],c:1,s:`The ray retraces its path only if it hits the silvered face normally.
Then the refraction angle at the first face equals the prism angle, r = 30\xBA.
Snell's law: sin i = \u03BC sin r = \u221A2 \xD7 sin 30\xBA = \u221A2 \xD7 (1)/(2) = (1)/(\u221A2).
So i = 45\xBA.`,fig:{w:300,h:240,alt:"Prism of apex angle 30 degrees with the right face silvered; a ray enters the left face at angle i and strikes the silvered face normally.",d:[["l",150,20,99.1,210,""],["l",150,20,200.9,210,""],["l",99.1,210,200.9,210,""],["l",153.6,33.5,161.6,31.3,""],["l",157.4,47.5,165.4,45.3,""],["l",161.1,61.5,169.1,59.3,""],["l",164.9,75.5,172.9,73.3,""],["l",168.6,89.5,176.6,87.3,""],["l",172.4,103.6,180.4,101.4,""],["l",176.1,117.6,184.1,115.4,""],["l",179.9,131.6,187.9,129.4,""],["l",183.6,145.6,191.6,143.4,""],["l",187.4,159.6,195.4,157.4,""],["l",191.2,173.6,199.2,171.4,""],["l",194.9,187.6,202.9,185.4,""],["l",43.5,185.2,117.1,142.7,""],["l",76.9,165.9,83.8,161.9,"a"],["l",117.1,142.7,178.5,126.3,""],["l",143.9,135.5,151.7,133.4,"a"],["l",80.4,132.9,146.1,150.5,"d"],["p","M92 136A26 26 0 0 0 94.6 155.7",""],["t",73.1,146.7,"i",""],["p","M136.5 147.9A20 20 0 0 0 136.5 137.5",""],["t",123.1,170.7,"30\xB0","s"],["p","M165.5 78A60 60 0 0 1 134.5 78",""],["t",150,100,"30\xB0","sm"],["p","M170.7 124.2L168.7 131.9L176.4 134",""],["t",150,198,"\u03BC = \u221A2","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2018-q003",pn:3,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Electromagnetic Induction",st:"Energy stored in inductor",q:"The magnetic potential energy stored in a certain inductor is 25 mJ, when the current in the inductor is 60 mA. This inductor is of inductance",o:["0.138 H","138.88 H","1.389 H","13.89 H"],c:3,s:`Energy stored U = (1)/(2)Li\xB2.
L = (2U)/(i\xB2) = (2 \xD7 25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)/((60 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2).
L = (50 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)/(3600 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = (500)/(36).
L = 13.89 H.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Induction"},{id:"neet-2018-q004",pn:4,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Image formation by concave mirror",q:"An object is placed at a distance of 40 cm from a concave mirror of focal length 15 cm. If the object is displaced through of distance of 20 cm towards the mirror, the displacement of the image will be",o:["30 cm away from the mirror","36 cm away from the mirror","30 cm towards the mirror","36 cm towards the mirror"],c:1,s:`Use the mirror formula (1)/(v) + (1)/(u) = (1)/(f) with f = \u201315 cm.
First position u = \u201340: (1)/(v) = (1)/(\u201315) + (1)/(40) = (\u20138 + 3)/(120) = \u2013(5)/(120), so v = \u201324 cm.
Object moves 20 cm closer, so u = \u201320: (1)/(v) = \u2013(1)/(15) + (1)/(20) = \u2013(1)/(60), so v = \u201360 cm.
Image shifts from 24 cm to 60 cm in front of the mirror.
Displacement = 60 \u2013 24 = 36 cm, away from the mirror.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2018-q005",pn:5,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"Logic gates combination",q:"In the combination of the following gates the output Y can be written in terms of inputs A and B as",o:["(A\xB7B)\u203E","A\xB7B\u0304 + \u0100\xB7B","(A\xB7B\u0304)\u203E + A\xB7B","(A+B)\u203E"],c:1,s:`Upper branch: A goes straight to an AND gate and B passes through a NOT gate, giving A\xB7B\u0304.
Lower branch: A passes through a NOT gate and B goes straight to an AND gate, giving \u0100\xB7B.
The OR gate adds them: Y = A\xB7B\u0304 + \u0100\xB7B.
This is the XOR function.`,fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"Gate circuit: A and NOT B feed an upper AND gate, NOT A and B feed a lower AND gate, and both AND outputs feed an OR gate giving Y.",d:[["c",20,41,2.6,"F"],["c",20,62,2.6,"F"],["t",12,45,"A","e"],["t",12,66,"B","e"],["l",20,41,150,41,""],["l",20,62,60,62,""],["c",60,62,2.6,"F"],["l",60,62,60,147,""],["l",60,147,150,147,""],["l",60,55,60,62,""],["l",60,55,75,55,""],["p","M75 55A5 5 0 0 1 85 55",""],["l",85,55,95,55,""],["p","M95 48L109 55L95 62Z",""],["c",111.5,55,2.5,""],["l",114,55,150,55,""],["c",80,41,2.6,"F"],["l",80,41,80,50,""],["l",80,60,80,133,""],["l",80,133,95,133,""],["p","M95 126L109 133L95 140Z",""],["c",111.5,133,2.5,""],["l",114,133,150,133,""],["p","M150 34V62H168A14 14 0 0 0 168 34Z",""],["p","M150 126V154H168A14 14 0 0 0 168 126Z",""],["p","M235 76Q255 76 271 90Q255 104 235 104Q245 90 235 76Z",""],["l",186,48,205,48,""],["l",205,48,205,83,""],["l",205,83,235,83,""],["l",186,140,205,140,""],["l",205,140,205,97,""],["l",205,97,235,97,""],["l",271,90,312,90,""],["c",312,90,2.6,"F"],["t",312,80,"Y","m"]]},sfig:{w:440,h:180,alt:"Same gate circuit with A\xB7B\u0304 and \u0100\xB7B labelled and the output Y = \u0100\xB7B + A\xB7B\u0304.",d:[["c",20,41,2.6,"F"],["c",20,62,2.6,"F"],["t",12,45,"A","e"],["t",12,66,"B","e"],["l",20,41,150,41,""],["l",20,62,60,62,""],["c",60,62,2.6,"F"],["l",60,62,60,147,""],["l",60,147,150,147,""],["l",60,55,60,62,""],["l",60,55,75,55,""],["p","M75 55A5 5 0 0 1 85 55",""],["l",85,55,95,55,""],["p","M95 48L109 55L95 62Z",""],["c",111.5,55,2.5,""],["l",114,55,150,55,""],["c",80,41,2.6,"F"],["l",80,41,80,50,""],["l",80,60,80,133,""],["l",80,133,95,133,""],["p","M95 126L109 133L95 140Z",""],["c",111.5,133,2.5,""],["l",114,133,150,133,""],["p","M150 34V62H168A14 14 0 0 0 168 34Z",""],["p","M150 126V154H168A14 14 0 0 0 168 126Z",""],["p","M235 76Q255 76 271 90Q255 104 235 104Q245 90 235 76Z",""],["l",186,48,205,48,""],["l",205,48,205,83,""],["l",205,83,235,83,""],["l",186,140,205,140,""],["l",205,140,205,97,""],["l",205,97,235,97,""],["l",271,90,312,90,""],["c",312,90,2.6,"F"],0,["t",192,43,"A\xB7B\u0304",""],["t",192,157,"\u0100\xB7B",""],["t",318,95,"Y = \u0100\xB7B + A\xB7B\u0304",""],["t",70,35,"A","m"],["t",103,70,"B\u0304","m"],["t",103,125,"\u0100","m"],["t",70,163,"B","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2018-q006",pn:6,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"Transistor as amplifier circuit",q:"In the circuit shown in the figure, the input voltage Vi is 20 V, VBE = 0 and VCE = 0. The values of IB, IC and \u03B2 are given by",o:["IB = 40 \xB5A, IC = 10 mA, \u03B2 = 250","IB = 25 \xB5A, IC = 5 mA, \u03B2 = 200","IB = 20 \xB5A, IC = 5 mA, \u03B2 = 250","IB = 40 \xB5A, IC = 5 mA, \u03B2 = 125"],c:3,s:`Input loop: 20 \u2013 IB \xD7 500 \xD7 10\xB3 \u2013 VBE = 0, with VBE = 0.
IB = (20)/(5 \xD7 10\u2075) = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 A = 40 \xB5A.
Output loop: 20 \u2013 IC \xD7 4 \xD7 10\xB3 \u2013 VCE = 0, with VCE = 0.
IC = (20)/(4 \xD7 10\xB3) = 5 mA.
\u03B2 = (IC)/(IB) = (5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)/(40 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 125.`,fig:{w:260,h:190,alt:"Common-emitter n-p-n transistor: 20 V supply through RC = 4 k\u03A9 to the collector, input Vi through RB = 500 k\u03A9 to the base, emitter grounded.",d:[["c",150,105,22,""],["l",139,92,139,118,""],["l",139,100,110,100,""],["l",139,98,155,84,""],["l",155,84,155,63,""],["l",139,110,154,123,"a"],["l",155,123,155,150,""],["p","M155 28L155 32.4L150 34.6L160 38.9L150 43.3L160 47.7L150 52.1L160 56.4L155 58.6L155 63",""],["c",155,24,2.6,"F"],["l",155,150,155,150,""],["l",155,150,155,158,""],["l",146,158,164,158,""],["l",149,162,161,162,""],["l",152,166,158,166,""],["t",155,14,"20V","m"],["t",165,86,"C","s"],["t",160,136,"E","s"],["t",121,120,"B","s"],["l",139,92,139,118,"B"],["c",40,100,2.6,"F"],["l",40,100,66,100,""],["p","M66 100L71 100L73.5 96L78.5 104L83.5 96L88.5 104L93.5 96L98.5 104L101 100L106 100",""],["l",106,100,139,100,""],["t",34,104,"V\u1D62","e"],["t",88,88,"R","e"],["t",89,91,"B","s"],["t",86,118,"500 k\u03A9","m"],["t",131,50,"R","e"],["t",132,53,"C","s"],["t",165,50,"4 k\u03A9",""]]},sfig:{w:260,h:190,alt:"The same circuit with the base current IB marked on the input side and loops drawn for the input and output sides.",d:[["c",150,105,22,""],["l",139,92,139,118,""],["l",139,100,110,100,""],["l",139,98,155,84,""],["l",155,84,155,63,""],["l",139,110,154,123,"a"],["l",155,123,155,150,""],["p","M155 28L155 32.4L150 34.6L160 38.9L150 43.3L160 47.7L150 52.1L160 56.4L155 58.6L155 63",""],["c",155,24,2.6,"F"],["l",155,150,155,150,""],["l",155,150,155,158,""],["l",146,158,164,158,""],["l",149,162,161,162,""],["l",152,166,158,166,""],["t",155,14,"20V","m"],["t",165,86,"C","s"],["t",160,136,"E","s"],["t",121,120,"B","s"],["l",139,92,139,118,"B"],["c",40,100,2.6,"F"],["l",40,100,66,100,""],["p","M66 100L71 100L73.5 96L78.5 104L83.5 96L88.5 104L93.5 96L98.5 104L101 100L106 100",""],["l",106,100,139,100,""],["l",48,88,100,88,"a"],["t",72,80,"I","e"],["t",73,83,"B","s"],["t",4,130,"V\u1D62 = 20V",""],["t",90,118,"500 k\u03A9","m"],["p","M52 140C82 140 108 152 120 178","a"],["l",200,40,200,168,"a"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2018-q007",pn:7,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"p-n junction diode characteristics",q:"In a p-n junction diode, change in temperature due to heating",o:["affects only reverse resistance","affects only forward resistance","does not affect resistance of p-n junction","affects the overall V \u2013 I characteristics of p-n junction"],c:3,s:`The diode current is I = I\u2080(e^(qV/kT) \u2013 1).
Both I\u2080 and the exponent depend on temperature T.
So heating changes both forward and reverse behaviour, that is the whole V\u2013I characteristic.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2018-q008",pn:8,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Terminal velocity and viscous force",q:"A small sphere of radius 'r' falls from rest in a viscous liquid. As a result, heat is produced due to viscous force. The rate of production of heat when the sphere attains its terminal velocity, is proportional to -",o:["r\xB3","r\xB2","r\u2075","r\u2074"],c:2,s:`Rate of heat production = power of the viscous force = F \xD7 v = 6\u03C0\u03B7rv \xD7 v = 6\u03C0\u03B7rv\xB2.
Terminal velocity v \u221D r\xB2.
So power \u221D r \xD7 (r\xB2)\xB2 = r\u2075.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids"},{id:"neet-2018-q009",pn:9,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Thermodynamics",st:"First law of thermodynamics",q:"A sample of 0.1 g of water at 100\xBAC and normal pressure (1.013 \xD7 10\u2075 Nm\u207B\xB2) requires 54 cal of heat energy to convert to steam at 100\xBAC. If the volume of the steam produced is 167.1 cc, the change in internal energy of the sample, is",o:["104.3 J","208.7 J","42.2 J","84.5 J"],c:1,s:`Heat given Q = 54 \xD7 4.18 = 225.72 J.
Initial volume of 0.1 g water = 0.1 cc.
Work done by the system W = P\u0394V = 1.013 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 (167.1 \u2013 0.1) \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 16.91 J.
First law: \u0394U = Q \u2013 W = 225.72 \u2013 16.91 = 208.7 J.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermodynamics"},{id:"neet-2018-q010",pn:10,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Solids",st:"Young's modulus and stretching",q:"Two wires are made of the same material and have the same volume. The first wire has cross-sectional area A and the second wire has cross-sectional area 3A. If the length of the first wire is increased by \u0394l on applying a force F, how much force is needed to stretch the second wire by the same amount ?",o:["9 F","6 F","4 F","F"],c:0,s:`Y = (F/A)/(\u0394l/l), so \u0394l = (Fl)/(AY).
Same volume V = Al, so l = V/A and \u0394l = (FV)/(A\xB2Y).
For wire 1: \u0394l = (FV)/(A\xB2Y). For wire 2 (area 3A): \u0394l = (F\u2032V)/(9A\xB2Y).
Equal \u0394l gives F\u2032 = 9F.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Mechanical Properties of Solids"},{id:"neet-2018-q011",pn:11,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Thermal Properties of Matter",st:"Wien's law and Stefan's law",q:"The power radiated by a black body is P and it radiates maximum energy at wavelength, \u03BB\u2080. If the temperature of the black body is now changed so that it radiates maximum energy at wavelength (3)/(4)\u03BB\u2080, the power radiated by it becomes nP. The value of n is",o:["(3)/(4)","(4)/(3)","(256)/(81)","(81)/(256)"],c:2,s:`Stefan's law: power \u221D T\u2074.
Wien's law: \u03BBT = constant, so T \u221D (1)/(\u03BB).
Hence power \u221D (1)/(\u03BB\u2074).
(nP)/(P) = (\u03BB\u2080/(3\u03BB\u2080/4))\u2074 = ((4)/(3))\u2074 = (256)/(81).
So n = (256)/(81).`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermal Properties of Matter"},{id:"neet-2018-q012",pn:12,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Current Electricity",st:"Series and parallel resistors with cell",q:"A set of 'n' equal resistors, of value 'R' each, are connected in series to a battery of emf 'E' and internal resistance 'R'. The current drawn is I. Now, the 'n' resistors are connected in parallel to the same battery. Then the current drawn from battery becomes 10 I. The value of 'n' is",o:["10","11","20","9"],c:0,s:`Series: I = (E)/(nR + R) = (E)/(R(n+1)).
Parallel: 10I = (E)/((R/n) + R) = (nE)/(R(n+1)).
Divide the first by the second: (1)/(10) = (1)/(n).
So n = 10.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Current Electricity"},{id:"neet-2018-q013",pn:13,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Current Electricity",st:"Cells in series, short circuit",q:"A battery consist of a variable number 'n' of identical cells (having internal resistance 'r' each) which are connected in series. The terminals of the battery are short-circuited and the current I is measured. Which of the graphs shows the correct relationship between I and n?",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:0,s:`n cells in series: emf = nE and internal resistance = nr.
Short circuit current I = (nE)/(nr) = (E)/(r).
This does not depend on n, so the graph of I against n is a horizontal line.
Note: The paper prints only a very short solution; this working expands it.`,fig:{w:340,h:250,alt:"Four graphs of I against n: (1) horizontal line, (2) straight line through the origin, (3) curve rising and flattening, (4) curve rising and steepening.",d:[["l",30,85,30,18,"a"],["l",30,85,140,85,"a"],["t",24,24,"I","e"],["t",144,89,"n",""],["t",24,98,"O","e"],["t",85,114,"(1)","m"],["l",190,85,190,18,"a"],["l",190,85,300,85,"a"],["t",184,24,"I","e"],["t",304,89,"n",""],["t",184,98,"O","e"],["t",245,114,"(2)","m"],["l",30,210,30,143,"a"],["l",30,210,140,210,"a"],["t",24,149,"I","e"],["t",144,214,"n",""],["t",24,223,"O","e"],["t",85,239,"(3)","m"],["l",190,210,190,143,"a"],["l",190,210,300,210,"a"],["t",184,149,"I","e"],["t",304,214,"n",""],["t",184,223,"O","e"],["t",245,239,"(4)","m"],["l",30,40,100,40,""],["l",190,85,265,30,""],["p","M30 210C38 183 60 165 90 158",""],["p","M190 210C220 209 240 190 252 148",""]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Current Electricity"},{id:"neet-2018-q014",pn:14,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Current Electricity",st:"Colour code of resistors",q:"A carbon resistor of (47 \xB1 4.7)k\u03A9 is to be marked with rings of different colours for its identification. The colour code sequence will be -",o:["Violet \u2013 Yellow \u2013 Orange \u2013 Silver","Yellow \u2013 Violet \u2013 Orange \u2013 Silver","Yellow \u2013 Green \u2013 Violet \u2013 Gold","Green \u2013 Orange \u2013 Violet \u2013 Gold"],c:1,s:`47 k\u03A9 = 47 \xD7 10\xB3 \u03A9: digits 4, 7 and multiplier 10\xB3.
Yellow = 4, Violet = 7, Orange = 10\xB3.
Tolerance = (4.7)/(47) \xD7 100% = 10%, which is the silver band.
Sequence: Yellow \u2013 Violet \u2013 Orange \u2013 Silver.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Current Electricity"},{id:"neet-2018-q015",pn:15,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Laws of Motion",st:"Friction",q:"Which one of the following statements is incorrect ?",o:["Rolling friction is smaller than sliding friction","Limiting value of static friction is directly proportional to normal reaction","Frictional force opposes the relative motion","Coefficient of sliding friction has dimensions of length"],c:3,s:`Friction f = \u03BCN, so \u03BC = f/N.
Both f and N are forces, so their dimensions cancel.
\u03BC is dimensionless, so statement (4) is incorrect.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Laws of Motion"},{id:"neet-2018-q016",pn:16,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Coefficient of restitution",q:"A moving block having mass m, collides with another stationary block having mass 4m. The lighter block comes to rest after collision. When the initial velocity of the lighter block is v, then the value of coefficient of restitution (e) will be",o:["0.5","0.25","0.8","0.4"],c:1,s:`Momentum conservation: mv = m \xD7 0 + 4m \xD7 v\u2032, so v\u2032 = (v)/(4).
e = (speed of separation)/(speed of approach) = ((v/4) \u2013 0)/(v \u2013 0) = (1)/(4).
e = 0.25.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Work, Energy and Power"},{id:"neet-2018-q017",pn:17,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Vertical circle, minimum height",q:"A body initially at rest and sliding along a frictionless track from a height h (as shown in the figure) just completes a vertical circle of diameter AB = D. height h is equal to",o:["(3)/(2)D","D","(7)/(5)D","(4)/(5)D"],c:3,s:`At the top B of the circle for just completing it: mg = (mv\xB2)/(r), so v\xB2 = rg = (gD)/(2).
Energy conservation from start to B (height D above the ground): mg(h \u2013 D) = (1)/(2)mv\xB2.
So g(h \u2013 D) = (1)/(2) \xD7 (gD)/(2), so h \u2013 D = (D)/(4).
This gives h = (5D)/(4).
Note: Printed answer 4 (4/5 D); my working gives h = 5D/4 (the printed option looks like a typo of 5/4 D). Printed key kept.`,fig:{w:280,h:190,alt:"A track from height h slopes down to point A at the bottom of a vertical circle of diameter AB = D, with B at the top.",d:[["l",40,20,40,160,"b"],["l",40,160,230,160,""],["l",40,20,150,160,""],["l",92,86,98,94,"a"],["c",230,137,23,""],["l",230,114,230,160,"d"],["t",46,95,"h",""],["t",230,106,"B","m"],["t",230,178,"A","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Work, Energy and Power"},{id:"neet-2018-q018",pn:18,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Moment of inertia and work",q:"Three objects, A : (a solid sphere), B : (a thin circular disk) and C : (a circular ring), each have the same mass M and radius R. They all spin with the same angular speed \u03C9 about their own symmetry axes. The amounts of work (W) required to bring them to rest would satisfy the relation",o:["WC > WB > WA","WA > WB > WC","WB > WA > WC","WA > WC > WB"],c:0,s:`Work needed = change in rotational kinetic energy = (1)/(2)I\u03C9\xB2.
\u03C9 is the same, so W \u221D I.
I(sphere) = (2)/(5)MR\xB2, I(disk) = (1)/(2)MR\xB2, I(ring) = MR\xB2.
So ring > disk > sphere: WC > WB > WA.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion"},{id:"neet-2018-q019",pn:19,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Waves",st:"Resonance tube",q:"A tuning fork is used to produce resonance in a glass tube. The length of the air column in this tube can be adjusted by a variable piston. At room temperature of 27\xB0C two successive resonance are produced at 20 cm and 73 cm of column length. If the frequency of the turning fork is 320 Hz, the velocity of sound in air at 27\xB0C is",o:["330 m/s","339 m/s","350 m/s","300 m/s"],c:1,s:`Two successive resonances differ by half a wavelength: l\u2082 \u2013 l\u2081 = (\u03BB)/(2).
(73 \u2013 20) cm = 0.53 m = (\u03BB)/(2), so \u03BB = 1.06 m.
v = f\u03BB = 320 \xD7 1.06 = 339 m/s.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Waves"},{id:"neet-2018-q020",pn:20,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Electric Charges and Fields",st:"Motion of charges in electric field",q:"A electron falls from rest through a vertical distance h in a uniform and vertically upward directed electric field E. The direction of electric field in now reversed, keeping its magnitude the same. A proton is allowed to fall from rest in it through the same vertical distance h. The time of fall of the electron, in comparison to the time of fall of the proton is",o:["smaller","5 times greater","10 times greater","equal"],c:0,s:`Force magnitude on each is eE, the same.
Acceleration a = (eE)/(m), so a \u221D (1)/(m).
Electron is much lighter, so its acceleration is larger.
From h = (1)/(2)at\xB2, t = \u221A((2h)/(a)), larger a means smaller t.
So the electron takes less time.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electric Charges and Fields"},{id:"neet-2018-q021",pn:21,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Simple harmonic motion",q:"A pendulum is hung from the roof of a sufficiently high building and is moving freely to and fro like a simple harmonic oscillator. The acceleration of the bob of the pendulum is 20 m/s\xB2 at a distance of 5 m from the mean position. The time period of oscillation is",o:["2\u03C0 s","\u03C0 s","2 s","1 s"],c:1,s:`In SHM |a| = \u03C9\xB2x.
20 = \u03C9\xB2 \xD7 5, so \u03C9\xB2 = 4 and \u03C9 = 2 rad/s.
T = (2\u03C0)/(\u03C9) = (2\u03C0)/(2) = \u03C0 s.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Oscillations"},{id:"neet-2018-q022",pn:22,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Force between capacitor plates",q:"The electrostatic force between the metal plates of an isolated parallel plate capacitor C having a charge Q and area A, is",o:["independent of the distance between the plates","linearly proportional to the distance between the plates","proportional to the square root of the distance between the plates","inversely proportional to the distance between the plates"],c:0,s:`Field due to one plate = (\u03C3)/(2\u03B5\u2080) = (Q)/(2A\u03B5\u2080).
Force on the other plate F = Q \xD7 (Q)/(2A\u03B5\u2080) = (Q\xB2)/(2A\u03B5\u2080).
This has no distance term, so the force is independent of the separation.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance"},{id:"neet-2018-q023",pn:23,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength",q:"An electron of mass m with an initial velocity V\u20D7 = V\u2080\xEE (V\u2080 > 0) enters an electric field E\u20D7 = \u2212E\u2080\xEE (E\u2080 = constant > 0) at t = 0. If \u03BB\u2080 is its de-Broglie wavelength initially, then its de-Broglie wavelength at time t is",o:["(\u03BB\u2080)/(1 + (eE\u2080)/(mV\u2080)t)","\u03BB\u2080(1 + (eE\u2080)/(mV\u2080)t)","\u03BB\u2080t","\u03BB\u2080"],c:0,s:`Force on electron F\u20D7 = \u2013e(\u2013E\u2080\xEE) = eE\u2080\xEE, so a = (eE\u2080)/(m) along +\xEE.
Velocity at time t: v = V\u2080 + (eE\u2080t)/(m) = V\u2080(1 + (eE\u2080t)/(mV\u2080)).
\u03BB = (h)/(mv) = (h)/(mV\u2080(1 + (eE\u2080t)/(mV\u2080))).
Since \u03BB\u2080 = (h)/(mV\u2080), \u03BB = (\u03BB\u2080)/(1 + (eE\u2080t)/(mV\u2080)).`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter"},{id:"neet-2018-q024",pn:24,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Radioactive decay law",q:"For a radioactive material, half-life is 10 minutes. If initially there are 600 number of nuclei, the time taken (in minutes) for the disintegration of 540 nuclei is",o:["20","10","30","15"],c:0,s:`The printed working takes 450 nuclei as disintegrated, so 150 remain.
600 \u2192 300 after one half-life \u2192 150 after two half-lives.
Time = 2 \xD7 10 = 20 minutes.
Note: Printed answer 1 (20 min). Disintegration of 540 would leave 60 nuclei (about 3.3 half-lives, about 33 min); the key fits 450 nuclei disintegrated. Printed key kept.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Nuclei"},{id:"neet-2018-q025",pn:25,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Photoelectric effect",q:"When the light of frequency 2\u03BD\u2080 (where \u03BD\u2080 is threshold frequency), is incident on a metal plate, the maximum velocity of electrons emitted is v\u2081. When the frequency of the incident radiation is increased to 5\u03BD\u2080, the maximum velocity of electrons emitted from the same plate is v\u2082. The ratio of v\u2081 to v\u2082 is",o:["1 : 2","1 : 4","4 : 1","2 : 1"],c:0,s:`Work function \u03C6 = h\u03BD\u2080.
First case: 2h\u03BD\u2080 = h\u03BD\u2080 + (1)/(2)mv\u2081\xB2, so (1)/(2)mv\u2081\xB2 = h\u03BD\u2080.
Second case: 5h\u03BD\u2080 = h\u03BD\u2080 + (1)/(2)mv\u2082\xB2, so (1)/(2)mv\u2082\xB2 = 4h\u03BD\u2080.
Divide: (v\u2081/v\u2082)\xB2 = (1)/(4), so v\u2081 : v\u2082 = 1 : 2.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter"},{id:"neet-2018-q026",pn:26,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Atoms",st:"Bohr model energies",q:"The ratio of kinetic energy to the total energy of an electron in a Bohr orbit of the hydrogen atom is",o:["1 : 1","1 : \u20131","2 : \u20131","1 : \u20132"],c:1,s:`In a Bohr orbit, kinetic energy KE = (kZe\xB2)/(2r).
Potential energy = \u2013(kZe\xB2)/(r), so total energy E = KE + PE = \u2013(kZe\xB2)/(2r).
KE : E = (kZe\xB2)/(2r) : \u2013(kZe\xB2)/(2r) = 1 : \u20131.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Atoms"},{id:"neet-2018-q027",pn:27,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Torque as cross product",q:"The moment of the force F\u20D7 = 4\xEE + 5\u0135 \u2212 6k\u0302 at (2, 0, \u20133), about the point (2, \u20132, \u20132), is given by",o:["\u22128\xEE \u2212 4\u0135 \u2212 7k\u0302","\u22124\xEE \u2212 \u0135 \u2212 8k\u0302","\u22127\xEE \u2212 8\u0135 \u2212 4k\u0302","\u22127\xEE \u2212 4\u0135 \u2212 8k\u0302"],c:3,s:`Position of the force point relative to the reference point: r\u20D7 = (2\u20132)\xEE + (0+2)\u0135 + (\u20133+2)k\u0302 = 2\u0135 \u2212 k\u0302.
\u03C4\u20D7 = r\u20D7 \xD7 F\u20D7 = determinant of rows (\xEE \u0135 k\u0302), (0 2 \u22121), (4 5 \u22126).
\xEE: (2)(\u20136) \u2013 (\u20131)(5) = \u201312 + 5 = \u20137.
\u0135: \u2013[(0)(\u20136) \u2013 (\u20131)(4)] = \u20134.
k\u0302: (0)(5) \u2013 (2)(4) = \u20138.
\u03C4\u20D7 = \u22127\xEE \u2212 4\u0135 \u2212 8k\u0302.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion"},{id:"neet-2018-q028",pn:28,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Laws of Motion",st:"Pseudo force on an inclined wedge",q:"A block of mass m is placed on a smooth inclined wedge ABC of inclination \u03B8 as shown in the figure. The wedge is given an acceleration 'a' towards the right. The relation between a and \u03B8 for the block to remain stationary on the wedge is",o:["a = (g)/(cosec\u03B8)","a = (g)/(sin\u03B8)","a = g cos\u03B8","a = g tan\u03B8"],c:3,s:`In the wedge's frame the block feels a pseudo force ma to the left.
Along the incline: mg sin\u03B8 (down the slope) is balanced by ma cos\u03B8 (up the slope).
mg sin\u03B8 = ma cos\u03B8, so a = g tan\u03B8.`,fig:{w:310,h:190,alt:"Wedge ABC with vertical side AC, base CB and angle theta at B; a block of mass m rests on the slope and the wedge accelerates to the right with acceleration a.",d:[["l",70,150,250,150,""],["l",100,30,100,150,""],["l",100,30,250,150,""],["l",104,150,168.9,85.1,""],["l",113,150,173.9,89.1,""],["l",122,150,178.9,93.1,""],["l",131,150,183.9,97.1,""],["l",140,150,188.9,101.1,""],["l",149,150,193.9,105.1,""],["l",158,150,198.9,109.1,""],["l",167,150,203.9,113.1,""],["l",176,150,208.9,117.1,""],["l",185,150,213.9,121.1,""],["l",194,150,218.9,125.1,""],["l",248,150,248.9,149.1,""],["l",100,142,162.2,79.8,""],["l",100,134,157.8,76.2,""],["l",100,126,153.3,72.7,""],["l",100,118,148.9,69.1,""],["l",100,110,144.4,65.6,""],["l",100,102,140,62,""],["l",100,94,135.6,58.4,""],["l",100,86,131.1,54.9,""],["l",100,78,126.7,51.3,""],["l",100,70,122.2,47.8,""],["l",100,62,117.8,44.2,""],["l",100,54,113.3,40.7,""],["l",100,46,108.9,37.1,""],["l",100,38,104.4,33.6,""],["p","M171.9 87.5L200 110L213.8 92.8L185.6 70.3Z","f"],["t",205.9,68.8,"m",""],["l",219,125,268,125,"a"],["t",274,129,"a",""],["p","M220 150A30 30 0 0 1 226.6 131.2",""],["t",220,143,"\u03B8","s"],["t",94,30,"A","e"],["t",100,168,"C","m"],["t",250,168,"B","m"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Free-body diagram of the block on the wedge: N perpendicular to the slope, mg sin\u03B8 down the slope, ma cos\u03B8 up the slope, and mg cos\u03B8 and ma sin\u03B8 into the slope.",d:[["l",70,170,270,170,""],["l",100,40,100,170,""],["l",100,40,270,170,""],["p","M166.7 91L192.1 110.5L205.5 93L180.1 73.6Z",""],["l",186.7,91.2,214.1,55.5,"a"],["l",186.7,91.2,147,60.8,"a"],["l",186.7,91.2,226.4,121.6,"a"],["l",186.7,91.2,161.2,124.6,"a"],["t",220.1,53.5,"N",""],["t",139,48.8,"ma cos\u03B8",""],["t",234.4,131.6,"mg sin\u03B8",""],["t",153.2,122.6,"ma sin\u03B8","e"],["t",165.2,140.6,"mg cos\u03B8","e"],["l",262,52,305,52,"a"],["t",311,56,"a",""],["p","M240 170A30 30 0 0 1 246.2 151.8",""],["t",241,166,"\u03B8","s"],["t",100,188,"C","m"],["t",270,188,"B","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Laws of Motion"},{id:"neet-2018-q029",pn:29,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Average velocity and average speed",q:"A toy car with charge q moves on a frictionless horizontal plane surface under the influence of a uniform electric field E\u20D7. Due to the force qE\u20D7 its velocity increases from 0 to 6 m/s in one second duration. At that instant the direction of the field is reversed. The car continues to move for two more seconds under the influence of this field. The average velocity and the average speed of the toy car between 0 to 3 seconds are respectively",o:["2 m/s, 4 m/s","1 m/s, 3 m/s","1 m/s, 3.5 m/s","1.5 m/s, 3 m/s"],c:1,s:`First second: a = 6 m/s\xB2, distance = (1)/(2) \xD7 6 \xD7 1\xB2 = 3 m, forward.
Field reversed: acceleration \u20136 m/s\xB2. Speed 6 m/s drops to 0 in 1 s, distance 3 m forward.
Third second: starts at rest, accelerates backward, distance 3 m backward.
Total path = 3 + 3 + 3 = 9 m; net displacement = 3 + 3 \u2013 3 = 3 m.
Average velocity = (3)/(3) = 1 m/s; average speed = (9)/(3) = 3 m/s.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Motion in a Straight Line"},{id:"neet-2018-q030",pn:30,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Screw gauge",q:"A student measured the diameter of a small steel ball using a screw gauge of least count 0.001 cm. The main scale reading is 5 mm and zero of circular scale division coincides with 25 division above the reference level. If screw gauge has a zero error of \u2013 0.004 cm, the correct diameter of the ball is",o:["0.521 cm","0.525 cm","0.053 cm","0.529 cm"],c:3,s:`Least count = 0.001 cm = 0.01 mm.
Circular scale reading = 25 \xD7 0.01 = 0.25 mm.
Observed reading = 5 + 0.25 = 5.25 mm.
Zero error is \u20130.004 cm = \u20130.04 mm, so correction = +0.04 mm.
Correct diameter = 5.25 + 0.04 = 5.29 mm = 0.529 cm.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Units and Measurements"},{id:"neet-2018-q031",pn:31,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Polarisation by reflection, Brewster's law",q:"Unpolarised light is incident from air on a plane surface of a material of refractive index '\u03BC'. At a particular angle of incidence 'i', it is found that the reflected and refracted rays are perpendicular to each other. Which of the following options is correct for this situation ?",o:["Reflected light is polarised with its electric vector parallel to the plane of incidence","Reflected light is polarised with its electric vector perpendicular to the plane of incidence","i = sin\u207B\xB9((1)/(\u03BC))","i = tan\u207B\xB9((1)/(\u03BC))"],c:1,s:`Reflected and refracted rays at 90\xB0 is the condition of Brewster's angle.
At this angle the reflected light is completely plane polarised.
Its electric vector is perpendicular to the plane of incidence.
Also tan i = \u03BC, so i = tan\u207B\xB9(\u03BC), which rules out options (3) and (4).`,sfig:{w:260,h:195,alt:"Unpolarised light incident on a surface between air and a medium; reflected and refracted rays are perpendicular, reflected ray polarised.",d:[["l",10,100,240,100,""],["t",235,86,"Air","e"],["t",235,120,"Medium (\u03BC)","e"],["l",23,41.3,110,100,""],["l",82.3,81.3,88.9,85.8,"a"],["l",110,100,192.9,44.1,""],["l",177.2,54.7,183.8,50.2,"a"],["l",110,100,163.1,178.8,""],["l",152.9,163.6,157.4,170.3,"a"],["c",94.3,89.4,2.2,"F"],["l",89.3,96.9,99.4,82,""],["p","M89.3 96.9L93.2 94.7L89.9 92.5Z","F"],["p","M99.4 82L95.5 84.2L98.8 86.4Z","F"],["c",75.2,76.5,2.2,"F"],["l",70.1,84,80.2,69.1,""],["p","M70.1 84L74 81.8L70.7 79.5Z","F"],["p","M80.2 69.1L76.3 71.3L79.6 73.5Z","F"],["c",134.9,83.2,2.2,"F"],["c",155.6,69.2,2.2,"F"],["c",121.7,117.3,2.2,"F"],["l",114.2,122.4,129.1,112.3,""],["p","M114.2 122.4L118.7 121.8L116.4 118.5Z","F"],["p","M129.1 112.3L124.7 112.9L127 116.2Z","F"],["c",136.6,139.4,2.2,"F"],["l",129.1,144.4,144,134.3,""],["p","M129.1 144.4L133.5 143.8L131.3 140.5Z","F"],["p","M144 134.3L139.6 134.9L141.8 138.2Z","F"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Wave Optics"},{id:"neet-2018-q032",pn:32,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Fringe angular width",q:"In Young's double slit experiment the separation d between the slits is 2 mm, the wavelength \u03BB of the light used is 5896 \xC5 and distance D between the screen and slits is 100 cm. It is found that the angular width of the fringes is 0.20\xB0. To increase the fringe angular width to 0.21\xB0 (with same \u03BB and D) the separation between the slits needs to be changed to",o:["1.8 mm","1.9 mm","2.1 mm","1.7 mm"],c:1,s:`Angular fringe width \u03B8 = (\u03BB)/(d), so \u03B8 \u221D (1)/(d) for fixed \u03BB.
(0.20\xB0)/(0.21\xB0) = (d\u2081)/(d), where d = 2 mm.
d\u2081 = (0.20 \xD7 2)/(0.21) = (0.40)/(0.21) = 1.9 mm.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Wave Optics"},{id:"neet-2018-q033",pn:33,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Astronomical telescope",q:"An astronomical refracting telescope will have large angular magnification and high angular resolution, when it has an objective lens of",o:["small focal length and large diameter","large focal length and small diameter","large focal length and large diameter","small focal length and small diameter"],c:2,s:`Magnifying power = (f\u2080)/(f\u2091), so a large objective focal length f\u2080 gives high magnification.
Resolving power = (a)/(1.22\u03BB), so a large aperture (diameter) a gives high resolution.
So the objective needs both a large focal length and a large diameter.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2018-q034",pn:34,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Thermodynamics",st:"Work and heat in isobaric process",q:"The volume (V) of a monatomic gas varies with its temperature (T), as shown in the graph. The ratio of work done by the gas, to the heat absorbed by it, when it undergoes a change form state A to stage B, is",o:["(2)/(5)","(2)/(3)","(1)/(3)","(2)/(7)"],c:0,s:`The graph of V against T is a straight line through the origin, so V \u221D T and the pressure is constant (isobaric).
W = nR\u0394T and Q = nCp\u0394T, so (W)/(Q) = (R)/(Cp).
For a monatomic gas Cp = (5)/(2)R.
(W)/(Q) = (R)/((5/2)R) = (2)/(5).`,fig:{w:260,h:185,alt:"Graph of V against T: a straight line from the origin O rising to the right, with points A and B on it and an arrow from A to B.",d:[["l",60,150,60,20,"a"],["l",60,150,230,150,"a"],["t",54,24,"V","e"],["t",236,154,"T",""],["t",50,164,"O","e"],["l",60,150,210,70,""],["l",124,115.5,162,95,"a"],["c",120,118,2.6,"F"],["c",165,94,2.6,"F"],["t",108,108,"A","e"],["t",168,84,"B",""]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermodynamics"},{id:"neet-2018-q035",pn:35,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Waves",st:"Organ pipes",q:"The fundamental frequency in an open organ pipe is equal to the third harmonic of a closed organ pipe. If the length of the closed organ pipe is 20 cm, the length of the open organ pipe is",o:["13.2 cm","8 cm","12.5 cm","16 cm"],c:0,s:`Open pipe fundamental: f = (v)/(2l\u2081).
Closed pipe third harmonic (first overtone): f\u2083 = (3v)/(4l\u2082), with l\u2082 = 20 cm.
Equate: (v)/(2l\u2081) = (3v)/(4l\u2082), so l\u2081 = (2l\u2082)/(3) = (40)/(3) = 13.3 cm.
The nearest option is 13.2 cm.
Note: My working gives 13.3 cm; option 1 (13.2 cm) is the nearest and matches the printed key.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Waves"},{id:"neet-2018-q036",pn:36,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Thermodynamics",st:"Efficiency of Carnot engine",q:"The efficiency of an ideal heat engine working between the freezing point and boiling point of water, is",o:["26.8%","20%","6.25%","12.5%"],c:0,s:`Sink T\u2082 = 0\xB0C = 273 K and source T\u2081 = 100\xB0C = 373 K.
\u03B7 = 1 \u2013 (T\u2082)/(T\u2081) = 1 \u2013 (273)/(373) = 1 \u2013 0.732 = 0.268.
Efficiency = 26.8%.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermodynamics"},{id:"neet-2018-q037",pn:37,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Kinetic Theory",st:"rms speed and escape velocity",q:"At what temperature will the rms speed of oxygen molecules become just sufficient for escaping from the Earth's atmosphere ? (Given : Mass of oxygen molecule (m) = 2.76 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2076 kg, Boltzmann' constant kB = 1.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 J K\u207B\xB9)",o:["2.508 \xD7 10\u2074 K","8.360 \xD7 10\u2074 K","5.016 \xD7 10\u2074 K","1.254 \xD7 10\u2074 K"],c:1,s:`rms speed v = \u221A((3kBT)/(m)), so T = (mv\xB2)/(3kB).
Escape speed of Earth = 11.2 km/s = 11.2 \xD7 10\xB3 m/s.
T = (2.76 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2076 \xD7 (11.2 \xD7 10\xB3)\xB2)/(3 \xD7 1.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3).
T = (2.76 \xD7 125.44 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2078)/(4.14 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3) \u2248 8.36 \xD7 10\u2074 K.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Kinetic Theory"},{id:"neet-2018-q038",pn:38,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Force on current-carrying rod",q:"A metallic rod of mass per unit length 0.5 kg m\u207B\xB9 is lying horizontally on a smooth inclined plane which makes an angle of 30\xB0 with the horizontal. The rod is not allowed to slide down by flowing a current through it when a magnetic field of induction 0.25 T is acting on it in the vertical direction. The current flowing in the rod to keep it stationary is",o:["7.14 A","5.98 A","14.76 A","11.32 A"],c:3,s:`The vertical field gives a horizontal force BIl on the rod. Its component up the slope is BIl cos\u03B8.
Balance with the weight component down the slope: mg sin\u03B8 = BIl cos\u03B8.
I = (mg tan\u03B8)/(Bl) = ((m/l) g tan\u03B8)/(B) = (0.5 \xD7 9.8 \xD7 tan30\xB0)/(0.25).
With tan30\xB0 = (1)/(\u221A3): I = (0.5 \xD7 9.8)/(0.25 \xD7 1.732) = 11.32 A.`,sfig:{w:340,h:195,alt:"Side view of an incline at angle theta: rod shown as a dot with current out of the page, vertical field B, force BIl with component BIl cos\u03B8 up the slope and mg sin\u03B8 down the slope.",d:[["l",60,170,290,170,""],["l",138.4,82.5,290,170,""],["c",194.7,115,5,""],["c",194.7,115,1.4,"F"],["t",203.7,108,"I",""],["l",194.7,108,194.7,53,"da"],["t",199.7,55,"B",""],["l",188.7,111.5,153.2,91,"da"],["t",132,70,"BI\u2113 sin(90\xB0 \u2212 \u03B8)","e"],["t",132,86,"BI\u2113 cos\u03B8","e"],["l",200.8,118.5,243.2,143,"da"],["t",256.7,133,"mg sin\u03B8",""],["l",194.7,122,194.7,165,"d"],["p","M194.7 135A20 20 0 0 0 204.7 132.3",""],["t",190.7,159,"(90\xB0 \u2212 \u03B8)","es"],["p","M260 170A30 30 0 0 1 264 155",""],["t",256,162,"\u03B8","e"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Moving Charges and Magnetism"},{id:"neet-2018-q039",pn:39,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Power in series LCR circuit",q:"An inductor 20 mH, a capacitor 100 \u03BCF and a resistor 50 \u03A9 are connected in series across a source of emf, V = 10 sin 314 t. The power loss in the circuit is",o:["0.79 W","0.43 W","2.74 W","1.13 W"],c:0,s:`\u03C9 = 314 rad/s, V\u2080 = 10 V.
XL = \u03C9L = 314 \xD7 20 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 6.28 \u03A9.
XC = (1)/(\u03C9C) = (1)/(314 \xD7 100 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 31.85 \u03A9.
Z\xB2 = R\xB2 + (XL \u2013 XC)\xB2 = 2500 + (25.57)\xB2 = 2500 + 653.8 = 3153.8.
P = (V\u2080\xB2R)/(2Z\xB2) = (100 \xD7 50)/(2 \xD7 3153.8) = 0.79 W.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Alternating Current"},{id:"neet-2018-q040",pn:40,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Magnetism and Matter",st:"Diamagnetism and energy",q:"A thin diamagnetic rod is placed vertically between the poles of an electromagnet. When the current in the electromagnetic is switched on, then the diamagnetic rod is pushed up, out of the horizontal magnetic field. Hence the rod gains gravitational potential energy. The work required to do this comes from",o:["the current source","the magnetic field","the lattice structure of the material of the rod","the induced electric field due to the changing magnetic field"],c:0,s:`A magnetic field itself does no work and the rod's lattice is not an energy source.
The energy stored in the electromagnet comes from the current source, which supplies the work for the gain in gravitational potential energy.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Magnetism and Matter"},{id:"neet-2018-q041",pn:41,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Galvanometer sensitivity",q:"Current sensitivity of a moving coil galvanometer is 5 div/mA and its voltage sensitivity (angular deflection per unit voltage applied) is 20 div/V. The resistance of the galvanometer is",o:["40 \u03A9","25 \u03A9","250 \u03A9","500 \u03A9"],c:2,s:`Current sensitivity Is = (\u03B8)/(I) and voltage sensitivity Vs = (\u03B8)/(V).
Since V = IG, Vs = (Is)/(G), so G = (Is)/(Vs).
Is = 5 div/mA = 5000 div/A, Vs = 20 div/V.
G = (5000)/(20) = 250 \u03A9.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Moving Charges and Magnetism"},{id:"neet-2018-q042",pn:42,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Acceleration due to gravity",q:"If the mass of the Sun were ten times smaller and the universal gravitational constant were ten times larger in magnitude, which of the following is not correct ?",o:["Raindrops will fall faster","Walking on the ground would become more difficult","Time period of a simple pendulum on the Earth would decrease","'g' on the Earth will not change"],c:3,s:`g on Earth = (GMe)/(R\xB2) depends on G and Earth's mass, not on the Sun's mass.
G becomes 10 times larger, so g becomes 10 times larger.
Raindrops then fall faster and walking is harder, and T = 2\u03C0\u221A(l/g) decreases.
So statement (4) is the incorrect one.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Gravitation"},{id:"neet-2018-q043",pn:43,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Rolling motion energy",q:"A solid sphere is in rolling motion. In rolling motion a body possesses translational kinetic energy (Kt) as well as rotational kinetic energy (Kr) simultaneously. The ratio Kt : (Kt + Kr) for the sphere is",o:["7 : 10","5 : 7","10 : 7","2 : 5"],c:1,s:`Kt = (1)/(2)mv\xB2 and Kr = (1)/(2)I\u03C9\xB2 = (1)/(2)mv\xB2(K\xB2/R\xB2), where K is the radius of gyration.
Kt/(Kt + Kr) = (1)/(1 + K\xB2/R\xB2).
For a solid sphere K\xB2/R\xB2 = (2)/(5), so the ratio = (1)/(7/5) = (5)/(7).`,sub:"Physics",ch:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion"},{id:"neet-2018-q044",pn:44,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Kepler's laws and orbital speed",q:"The kinetic energies of a planet in an elliptical orbit about the Sun, at positions A, B and C are KA, KB and KC, respectively. AC is the major axis and SB is perpendicular to AC at the position of the Sun S as shown in the figure. Then",o:["KA < KB < KC","KA > KB > KC","KB < KA < KC","KB > KA > KC"],c:1,s:`A is the nearest point to the Sun and C is the farthest, so rA < rB < rC.
Angular momentum mvr is conserved, so speed is larger where r is smaller: vA > vB > vC.
Kinetic energy follows speed: KA > KB > KC.`,fig:{w:290,h:125,alt:"Ellipse with major axis AC, the Sun S on the axis nearer A, and SB perpendicular to AC with B on the ellipse.",d:[["p","M30 70A110 45 0 1 1 250 70A110 45 0 1 1 30 70Z",""],["l",30,70,250,70,""],["l",85,70,85,31,""],["c",85,70,2.6,"F"],["c",85,70,3.8,"F"],["p","M91 70V64H85",""],["t",24,74,"A","e"],["t",256,74,"C",""],["t",90,88,"S",""],["t",85,22,"B","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Gravitation"},{id:"neet-2018-q045",pn:45,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Conservation of angular momentum",q:"A solid sphere is rotating freely about its symmetry axis in free space. The radius of the sphere is increased keeping its mass same. Which of the following physical quantities would remain constant for the sphere ?",o:["Angular velocity","Moment of inertia","Rotational kinetic energy","Angular momentum"],c:3,s:`The sphere is in free space, so no external torque acts on it.
Therefore its angular momentum stays constant.
Moment of inertia increases with radius, so \u03C9 and kinetic energy change.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion"},{id:"neet-2018-q046",pn:46,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids",st:"Decomposition of formic and oxalic acid",q:"A mixture of 2.3 g formic acid and 4.5 g oxalic acid is treated with conc. H\u2082SO\u2084. The evolved gaseous mixture is passed through KOH pellets. Weight (in g) of the remaining product at STP will be",o:["1.4","3.0","2.8","4.4"],c:2,s:`Moles of HCOOH (46 g/mol) = (2.3)/(46) = 0.05 mol; moles of H\u2082C\u2082O\u2084 (90 g/mol) = (4.5)/(90) = 0.05 mol.
HCOOH \u2192 H\u2082O + CO gives 0.05 mol CO.
H\u2082C\u2082O\u2084 \u2192 H\u2082O + CO + CO\u2082 gives 0.05 mol CO and 0.05 mol CO\u2082.
Conc. H\u2082SO\u2084 absorbs water and KOH absorbs CO\u2082.
Remaining gas is CO = 0.05 + 0.05 = 0.1 mol, mass = 0.1 \xD7 28 = 2.8 g.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids"},{id:"neet-2018-q047",pn:47,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Amines",st:"Nitration of aniline",q:"Nitration of aniline in strong acidic medium also gives m-nitroaniline because",o:["In spite of substituents nitro group always goes to only m-position","In electrophilic substitution reactions amino group is meta directive","In absence of substituents nitro group always goes to m-position","In acidic (strong) medium aniline is present as anilinium ion"],c:3,s:`In strongly acidic nitrating mixture, aniline is protonated to the anilinium ion, C\u2086H\u2085NH\u2083\u207A.
The \u2013NH\u2083\u207A group has a strong \u2013I effect, so it is deactivating and meta directing.
This is why some m-nitroaniline forms.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Amines"},{id:"neet-2018-q048",pn:48,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Nature of oxides of group 2",q:"Which of the following oxides is most acidic in nature?",o:["MgO","BeO","BaO","CaO"],c:1,s:`Basic strength of the oxide increases as the metal's ionisation energy decreases.
Ionisation energy falls down the group: Be > Mg > Ca > Ba.
So basic nature is BeO < MgO < CaO < BaO.
BeO is the least basic, so the most acidic (actually amphoteric).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The s-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2018-q049",pn:49,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Starch: amylose and amylopectin",q:"The difference between amylose and amylopectin is",o:["Amylopectin have 1 \u2192 4 \u03B1-linkage and 1 \u2192 6 \u03B1-linkage","Amylose have 1 \u2192 4 \u03B1-linkage and 1 \u2192 6 \u03B2-linkage","Amylopectin have 1 \u2192 4 \u03B1-linkage and 1 \u2192 6 \u03B2 linkage","Amylose is made up of glucose and galactose"],c:0,s:`Amylose is a long unbranched chain of \u03B1-D-glucose joined by 1\u21924 \u03B1-glycosidic links.
Amylopectin is branched: main chain has 1\u21924 \u03B1-links and branches start through 1\u21926 \u03B1-links.
Both are made of glucose only, so only option (1) is correct.`,sfig:{w:315,h:195,alt:"Amylopectin: glucose rings joined by alpha-1,4 links in chains, with a branch from carbon 1 through O to CH2 (carbon 6) of a ring in the second chain, an alpha-1,6 link.",d:[["l",62,40,51,59.1,""],["l",51,59.1,29,59.1,""],["l",29,59.1,18,40,""],["l",18,40,29,20.9,""],["l",29,20.9,43,20.9,""],["l",62,40,55,27.9,""],["t",51,24.9,"O","m"],["l",132,40,121,59.1,""],["l",121,59.1,99,59.1,""],["l",99,59.1,88,40,""],["l",88,40,99,20.9,""],["l",99,20.9,113,20.9,""],["l",132,40,125,27.9,""],["t",121,24.9,"O","m"],["l",202,40,191,59.1,""],["l",191,59.1,169,59.1,""],["l",169,59.1,158,40,""],["l",158,40,169,20.9,""],["l",169,20.9,183,20.9,""],["l",202,40,195,27.9,""],["t",191,24.9,"O","m"],["l",62,40,69,40,""],["l",81,40,88,40,""],["t",75,44,"O","m"],["l",132,40,139,40,""],["l",151,40,158,40,""],["t",145,44,"O","m"],["l",95,150,84,169.1,""],["l",84,169.1,62,169.1,""],["l",62,169.1,51,150,""],["l",51,150,62,130.9,""],["l",62,130.9,76,130.9,""],["l",95,150,88,137.9,""],["t",84,134.9,"O","m"],["l",165,150,154,169.1,""],["l",154,169.1,132,169.1,""],["l",132,169.1,121,150,""],["l",121,150,132,130.9,""],["l",132,130.9,146,130.9,""],["l",165,150,158,137.9,""],["t",154,134.9,"O","m"],["l",235,150,224,169.1,""],["l",224,169.1,202,169.1,""],["l",202,169.1,191,150,""],["l",191,150,202,130.9,""],["l",202,130.9,216,130.9,""],["l",235,150,228,137.9,""],["t",224,134.9,"O","m"],["l",95,150,102,150,""],["l",114,150,121,150,""],["t",108,154,"O","m"],["l",165,150,172,150,""],["l",184,150,191,150,""],["t",178,154,"O","m"],["t",105,80,"\u03B1-1,4 linkage","m"],["l",202,40,202,66,""],["t",202,74,"O","m"],["l",202,78,202,95,""],["t",202,108,"CH\u2082","m"],["t",222,108,"6",""],["t",209,58,"1",""],["t",224,80,"\u03B1 linkage",""],["l",202,112,202,131,""],["l",8,150,28,150,""],["t",34,154,"O","m"],["l",40,150,51,150,""],["p","M16 126H10V174H16",""],["l",235,150,256,150,""],["t",262,154,"O","m"],["l",268,150,286,150,""],["p","M280 126H286V174H280",""],["t",292,178,"n","s"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2018-q050",pn:50,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Polymers",st:"Cross-linked polymers",q:"Regarding cross-linked or network polymers, which of the following statements is incorrect?",o:["They contain covalent bonds between various linear polymer chains.","They are formed from bi-and tri-functional monomers","Examples are bakelite and melamine","They contain strong covalent bonds in their polymer chains"],c:3,s:`Cross-linked polymers have strong covalent bonds linking the separate linear chains, and are made from bi- and tri-functional monomers (for example bakelite and melamine).
Statements (1), (2) and (3) are correct.
Statement (4) as worded is taken as the incorrect one, as per the printed key.
Note: Statement (4) looks true as written; printed key 4 kept.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Polymers"},{id:"neet-2018-q051",pn:51,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers",st:"Reimer-Tiemann reaction",q:"In the reaction phenol + CHCl\u2083 + NaOH \u2192 sodium salicylaldehyde (o-O\u207BNa\u207A on the ring and \u2013CHO ortho to it), the electrophile involved is",o:["dicloromethyl cation (C\u207AHCl\u2082)","formyl cation (C\u207AHO)","dichloromethyl anion (C\u207BHCl\u2082)","dichlorocarbene (:CCl\u2082)"],c:3,s:`This is the Reimer\u2013Tiemann reaction.
Hydroxide removes H\u207A from CHCl\u2083 to give \u207BCCl\u2083, which loses Cl\u207B to form dichlorocarbene :CCl\u2082.
:CCl\u2082 is electron-deficient, so it is the electrophile that attacks the phenoxide ring.`,fig:{w:340,h:110,alt:"Phenol reacts with CHCl3 and NaOH to give the sodium salt of salicylaldehyde (O-Na+ and CHO on adjacent ring carbons).",d:[["p","M40 56L53.9 64L53.9 80L40 88L26.1 80L26.1 64Z",""],["c",40,72,9.3,""],["l",40,56,40,46,""],["t",40,40,"OH","m"],["t",66,77,"+ CHCl\u2083 + NaOH",""],["l",168,72,218,72,"a"],["p","M262 56L275.9 64L275.9 80L262 88L248.1 80L248.1 64Z",""],["c",262,72,9.3,""],["l",262,56,262,46,""],["t",262,40,"O\u207B Na\u207A","m"],["l",275.9,64,289.9,56,""],["t",291.9,58,"CHO",""]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers"},{id:"neet-2018-q052",pn:52,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids",st:"Boiling points of carboxylic acids",q:"Carboxylic acids higher boiling points than aldehydes, ketones and even alcohols of comparable molecular mass. It is due to their",o:["formation of intramolecular H-bonding","formation of carboxylate ion","more extensive association of carboxylic acid via vander Waals force of attraction","formation of intermolecular H-bonding"],c:3,s:`Two carboxylic acid molecules join through two O\u2013H\xB7\xB7\xB7O hydrogen bonds to form a stable dimer.
These are intermolecular H-bonds, stronger and more extensive than in alcohols or in aldehydes and ketones (which cannot H-bond to themselves).
More energy is needed to separate them, so the boiling point is higher.`,sfig:{w:290,h:112,alt:"Carboxylic acid dimer: two R-COOH molecules joined by two O-H...O hydrogen bonds forming a six-membered ring, with delta charges on the atoms.",d:[["t",6,64,"R \u2013 C",""],["t",84,42,"O","m"],["t",79,23,"\u2013\u03B4","ms"],["t",132,23,"+\u03B4","ms"],["t",161,23,"\u2013\u03B4","ms"],["l",100,38,126,38,"d"],["t",136,42,"H","m"],["l",143,38,153,38,""],["t",166,42,"O","m"],["l",172,38,198,52,""],["t",204,58,"C \u2013 R",""],["l",54,56,78,43,""],["l",56,60,80,47,""],["l",55,67,78,80,""],["t",86,85,"O","m"],["l",95,81,105,81,""],["t",114,85,"H","m"],["l",124,81,162,81,"d"],["t",172,85,"O","m"],["l",202,63,183,76,""],["l",199,67,180,80,""],["t",82,100,"\u2013\u03B4","ms"],["t",129,100,"+\u03B4","ms"],["t",172,100,"\u2013\u03B4","ms"],["t",232,85,"(Dimer)",""]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids"},{id:"neet-2018-q053",pn:53,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers",st:"Haloform (iodoform) test",q:"Compound A, C\u2088H\u2081\u2080O, is found to react with NaOI (produced by reacting Y with NaOH) and yields a yellow precipitate with characteristic smell. A and Y are respectively",o:["4-methylbenzyl alcohol (CH\u2083\u2013C\u2086H\u2084\u2013CH\u2082\u2013OH) and I\u2082","2-phenylethanol (C\u2086H\u2085\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013OH) and I\u2082","1-phenylethanol (C\u2086H\u2085\u2013CH(OH)\u2013CH\u2083) and I\u2082","2,4-dimethylphenol (a ring with two CH\u2083 and one OH) and I\u2082"],c:2,s:`A yellow precipitate with a characteristic smell is iodoform, CHI\u2083 (haloform reaction).
NaOI is made from I\u2082 + NaOH, so Y = I\u2082.
Only compounds with the CH\u2083\u2013CH(OH)\u2013 group (or CH\u2083CO\u2013) give this test.
Of the options with formula C\u2088H\u2081\u2080O, only C\u2086H\u2085\u2013CH(OH)\u2013CH\u2083 has it.
It gives sodium benzoate and CHI\u2083.`,fig:{w:300,h:235,alt:"Four options: (1) 4-methylbenzyl alcohol with I2; (2) 2-phenylethanol with I2; (3) 1-phenylethanol with I2; (4) 2,4-dimethylphenol with I2.",d:[["t",6,29,"(1)",""],["p","M102 25L95 37.1L81 37.1L74 25L81 12.9L95 12.9Z",""],["c",88,25,8.1,""],["t",28,29,"H\u2083C",""],["l",56,25,74,25,""],["l",102,25,120,25,""],["t",122,29,"CH\u2082 \u2013 OH and I\u2082",""],["t",6,79,"(2)",""],["p","M74 75L67 87.1L53 87.1L46 75L53 62.9L67 62.9Z",""],["c",60,75,8.1,""],["l",74,75,86,75,""],["t",88,79,"CH\u2082 \u2013 CH\u2082 \u2013 OH and I\u2082",""],["t",6,134,"(3)",""],["p","M74 130L67 142.1L53 142.1L46 130L53 117.9L67 117.9Z",""],["c",60,130,8.1,""],["l",74,130,86,130,""],["t",88,134,"CH \u2013 CH\u2083 and I\u2082",""],["l",98,137,98,148,""],["t",98,162,"OH","m"],["t",6,214,"(4)",""],["p","M110 215L103 227.1L89 227.1L82 215L89 202.9L103 202.9Z",""],["c",96,215,8.1,""],["l",82,215,62,215,""],["t",32,219,"CH\u2083",""],["l",110,215,120,215,""],["t",122,219,"OH and I\u2082",""],["l",103,203,115,191,""],["t",112,187,"CH\u2083",""]]},sfig:{w:360,h:150,alt:"C6H5-CH(OH)-CH3 reacts with I2 and NaOH (or NaOI) to give C6H5-COONa and CHI3, a yellow precipitate.",d:[["p","M25 76L38.9 84L38.9 100L25 108L11.1 100L11.1 84Z",""],["c",25,92,9.3,""],["l",25,76,25,52,""],["l",25,36,25,28,""],["t",25,24,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",25,48,"CH \u2013 OH","m"],["t",33,134,"(A)","m"],["t",121,84,"I\u2082 + NaOH","m"],["l",60,92,208,92,"a"],["t",121,110,"Or","m"],["t",121,128,"NaOI","m"],["t",136,58,"(Y)","m"],["l",112,72,124,62,"a"],["p","M232 76L245.9 84L245.9 100L232 108L218.1 100L218.1 84Z",""],["c",232,92,9.3,""],["l",232,76,232,56,""],["t",232,50,"COONa","m"],["t",256,96,"+ CHI\u2083",""],["t",298,134,"(yellow ppt)","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers"},{id:"neet-2018-q054",pn:54,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Order and half-life",q:"The correct difference between first and second order reactions is that",o:["the rate of a first-order reactions does not depend on reactant concentrations; the rate of a second-order reaction does depend on reactant concentrations","the half-life of a first-order reaction does not depend on [A]\u2080; the half-life of a second-order reaction does depend on [A]\u2080","a first-order reaction can be catalyzed; a second-order reaction cannot be catalyzed","the rate of a first-order reaction does depend on reactant concentrations; the rate a second-order reaction does not depend on reactant concentrations"],c:1,s:`For a first-order reaction t\xBD = (0.693)/(k), which has no [A]\u2080 in it.
For a second-order reaction t\xBD = (1)/(k[A]\u2080), which depends on [A]\u2080.
Rates of both depend on concentration, and both can be catalysed, so the other options are wrong.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Kinetics"},{id:"neet-2018-q055",pn:55,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Hydrogen",st:"Ionic and covalent hydrides",q:"Among CaH\u2082, BeH\u2082, BaH\u2082, the order of ionic character is",o:["BeH\u2082 < CaH\u2082 < BaH\u2082","CaH\u2082 < BeH\u2082 < BaH\u2082","BeH\u2082 < BaH\u2082 < CaH\u2082","BaH\u2082 < BeH\u2082 < CaH\u2082"],c:0,s:`Covalent character grows with the polarising power of the cation, which is larger for a smaller cation.
Size of cation: Be\xB2\u207A < Ca\xB2\u207A < Ba\xB2\u207A.
Polarisation falls in the same direction, so ionic character rises: BeH\u2082 < CaH\u2082 < BaH\u2082.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrogen"},{id:"neet-2018-q056",pn:56,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Redox Reactions",st:"Disproportionation from potentials",q:"Consider the change in oxidation state of Bromine corresponding to different emf values as shown in the diagram below : BrO\u2084\u207B \u2013(1.82 V)\u2192 BrO\u2083\u207B \u2013(1.5 V)\u2192 HBrO \u2013(1.595 V)\u2192 Br\u2082 \u2013(1.0652 V)\u2192 Br\u207B. Then the species undergoing disproportionation is",o:["BrO\u2083\u207B","BrO\u2084\u207B","Br\u2082","HBrO"],c:3,s:`A species disproportionates if it can be reduced to its right-hand neighbour and oxidised to its left-hand neighbour with a positive cell emf.
HBrO (+1) \u2192 Br\u2082 (0): reduction, E\xB0 = +1.595 V.
HBrO (+1) \u2192 BrO\u2083\u207B (+5): oxidation, E\xB0 = \u20131.5 V.
E\xB0cell = 1.595 \u2013 1.5 = +0.095 V, which is positive.
So HBrO disproportionates.`,fig:{w:460,h:50,alt:"Latimer diagram: BrO4- to BrO3- 1.82 V, BrO3- to HBrO 1.5 V, HBrO to Br2 1.595 V, Br2 to Br- 1.0652 V.",d:[["t",6,35,"BrO\u2084\u207B",""],["l",46,31,108,31,"a"],["t",77,24,"1.82V","ms"],["t",112,35,"BrO\u2083\u207B",""],["l",152,31,204,31,"a"],["t",178,24,"1.5V","ms"],["t",208,35,"HBrO",""],["l",246,31,322,31,"a"],["t",284,24,"1.595V","ms"],["t",326,35,"Br\u2082",""],["l",348,31,424,31,"a"],["t",386,24,"1.0652V","ms"],["t",428,35,"Br\u207B",""]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Redox Reactions"},{id:"neet-2018-q057",pn:57,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Mole concept",q:"In which case is the number of molecules of water maximum ?",o:["18 mL of water","0.18 g of water","0.00224 L of water vapours at 1 atm and 273 K","10\u207B\xB3 mol of water"],c:0,s:`Count moles in each case.
18 mL water = 18 g (density 1 g/mL) = 1 mol.
0.18 g = (0.18)/(18) = 0.01 mol.
0.00224 L at STP = (0.00224)/(22.4) = 10\u207B\u2074 mol.
10\u207B\xB3 mol is given directly.
The largest is 1 mol, so option 1.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2018-q058",pn:58,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure",st:"Ionic compound formulae",q:"Magnesium reacts with an element (X) to form an ionic compound. If the ground state electronic configuration of (X) is 1s\xB2 2s\xB2 2p\xB3, the simplest formula for this compound is",o:["Mg\u2082X\u2083","MgX\u2082","Mg\u2082X","Mg\u2083X\u2082"],c:3,s:`X has 5 valence electrons, so it gains 3 to form X\xB3\u207B (it is nitrogen).
Mg loses 2 electrons to form Mg\xB2\u207A.
Cross the charges: Mg\u2083X\u2082 (3 Mg\xB2\u207A = +6 balances 2 X\xB3\u207B = \u20136).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure"},{id:"neet-2018-q059",pn:59,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"The Solid State",st:"Density of bcc and fcc",q:"Iron exhibits bcc structure at room temperature. Above 900\xBAC, it transforms to fcc structure. The ratio of density of iron at room temperature to that at 900\xBAC (assuming molar mass and atomic radii of iron remain constant with temperature) is",o:["(\u221A3)/(\u221A2)","(4\u221A3)/(3\u221A2)","(3\u221A3)/(4\u221A2)","(1)/(2)"],c:2,s:`Density = (Z \xD7 M)/(N_A \xD7 a\xB3), and the atomic radius r is the same in both.
bcc: Z = 2 and a = (4r)/(\u221A3). fcc: Z = 4 and a = 2\u221A2 r.
\u03C1(bcc)/\u03C1(fcc) = (2)/(4) \xD7 (a_fcc/a_bcc)\xB3.
a_fcc/a_bcc = (2\u221A2 r \xD7 \u221A3)/(4r) = (\u221A6)/(2).
((\u221A6)/(2))\xB3 = (6\u221A6)/(8) = (3\u221A6)/(4).
Ratio = (1)/(2) \xD7 (3\u221A6)/(4) = (3\u221A6)/(8) = (3\u221A3)/(4\u221A2).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The Solid State"},{id:"neet-2018-q060",pn:60,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Structure of Atom",st:"Quantum numbers and Hund's rule",q:"Which one is a wrong statement ?",o:["Total orbital angular momentum of electron in 's' orbital is equal to zero","An orbital is designated by three quantum numbers while an electron in an atom is designated by four quantum numbers.","The electronic configuration of N atom is 1s\xB2 2s\xB2 2p\xB9\u2093 2p\xB9\u1D67 2p\xB9_z (orbital box diagram with spins shown)","The value of m for d_z\xB2 is zero"],c:2,s:`(1) For an s orbital l = 0, so orbital angular momentum = 0: true.
(2) An orbital needs n, l, m; an electron needs n, l, m, s: true.
(4) For d_z\xB2, m = 0: true.
(3) is wrong: in the boxes the third 2p electron has opposite spin (\u2193) to the other two. By Hund's rule, unpaired electrons in degenerate orbitals have the same spin.`,fig:{w:255,h:80,alt:"Orbital boxes: 1s2 up-down, 2s2 up-down, then 2px up, 2py up, 2pz down.",d:[["r",10,24,42,42,""],["r",63,24,42,42,""],["r",119,24,41,42,""],["r",160,24,41,42,""],["r",201,24,41,42,""],["l",25,60,25,32,"a"],["l",37,32,37,60,"a"],["l",78,60,78,32,"a"],["l",90,32,90,60,"a"],["l",139.5,60,139.5,32,"a"],["l",180.5,60,180.5,32,"a"],["l",221.5,32,221.5,60,"a"],["t",31,18,"1s\xB2","m"],["t",84,18,"2s\xB2","m"],["t",139.5,18,"2p_x\xB9","ms"],["t",180.5,18,"2p_y\xB9","ms"],["t",221.5,18,"2p_z\xB9","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Structure of Atom"},{id:"neet-2018-q061",pn:61,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure",st:"Bond order by MO theory",q:"Consider the following species : CN\u207A, CN\u207B, NO and CN. Which one of these will have the highest bond order ?",o:["NO","CN\u207B","CN\u207A","CN"],c:1,s:`Bond order = (Nb \u2013 Na)/(2).
CN\u207B has 14 electrons (like N\u2082): Nb = 10, Na = 4, bond order = (10 \u2013 4)/(2) = 3.
NO = 2.5, CN = 2.5 and CN\u207A = 2.
So CN\u207B has the highest bond order.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure"},{id:"neet-2018-q062",pn:62,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Halogens",q:"Which of the following statements is not true for halogens ?",o:["All form monobasic oxyacids","All are oxidizing agents","All but fluorine show positive oxidation states","Chlorine has the highest electron-gain enthalpy"],a:"Bonus",s:`Fluorine shows only \u20131 oxidation state, so (3) is true.
Chlorine has the most negative electron-gain enthalpy, so (4) is true.
All halogens are oxidising agents, so (2) is true.
Fluorine forms only HOF as an oxyacid, which makes (1) doubtful.
Note: Printed answer is Bonus (no single correct option kept).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2018-q063",pn:63,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Group 13 hexafluoro ions",q:"Which one of the following elements is unable to form MF\u2086\xB3\u207B ion ?",o:["Ga","Al","B","In"],c:2,s:`MF\u2086\xB3\u207B needs the central atom to use d orbitals (sp\xB3d\xB2 hybridisation).
Boron is in period 2 and has no d orbitals, so it cannot form BF\u2086\xB3\u207B.
Al, Ga and In have vacant d orbitals and can form it.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2018-q064",pn:64,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure",st:"VSEPR shape of ClF\u2083",q:"In the structure of ClF\u2083, the number of lone pairs of electrons on central atom 'Cl' is",o:["one","two","four","three"],c:1,s:`Cl has 7 valence electrons; three are used to bond with three F atoms.
The remaining 4 electrons form 2 lone pairs.
Steric number is 5, so sp\xB3d hybridisation, giving a T-shaped molecule.`,sfig:{w:420,h:125,alt:"ClF3 T-shaped with two lone pairs on Cl, and the sp3d trigonal bipyramid view with lone pairs in equatorial positions.",d:[["t",3,53,"ClF\u2083 \u21D2",""],["c",95,36,1.3,"F"],["c",101,36,1.3,"F"],["c",88,44,1.3,"F"],["c",88,50,1.3,"F"],["t",99,51,"Cl","m"],2,3,4,5,["l",107,47,137,47,""],["t",146,51,"F",""],["l",92,56,84,78,""],["t",79,92,"F","m"],["l",103,57,103,80,""],["t",103,92,"F","m"],["t",246,53,"sp\xB3d",""],["t",349,67,"Cl","m"],["l",349,30,349,56,""],["t",349,24,"F","m"],["l",349,76,349,98,""],["t",349,111,"F","m"],["l",358,63,386,63,""],["t",394,67,"F",""],["c",318,44,1.3,"F"],["c",321,51,1.3,"F"],["c",321,76,1.3,"F"],["c",318,83,1.3,"F"],["l",340,62,322,52,""],["l",340,66,322,78,""],25,26,27,28]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure"},{id:"neet-2018-q065",pn:65,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Ellingham diagram",q:"Considering Ellingham diagram, which of the following metals can be used to reduce alumina ?",o:["Fe","Zn","Mg","Cu"],c:2,s:`On an Ellingham diagram a metal can reduce the oxide of another metal if its own oxide line lies below that of the oxide.
The Mg \u2192 MgO line is below the Al \u2192 Al\u2082O\u2083 line.
Fe, Zn and Cu lie above, so only Mg can reduce alumina.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2018-q066",pn:66,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Atomic radii of group 13",q:"The correct order of atomic radii in group 13 elements is",o:["B < Al < In < Ga < Tl","B < Al < Ga < In < Tl","B < Ga < Al < Tl < In","B < Ga < Al < In < Tl"],c:3,s:`Radius normally increases down the group.
Ga is slightly smaller than Al because the 3d electrons shield poorly, so Zeff is higher.
Order: B < Ga < Al < In < Tl.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2018-q067",pn:67,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Redox Reactions",st:"Oxidation numbers of nitrogen",q:"The correct order of N-compounds in its decreasing order of oxidation states is",o:["HNO\u2083, NO, N\u2082, NH\u2084Cl","HNO\u2083, NO, NH\u2084Cl, N\u2082","HNO\u2083, NH\u2084Cl, NO, N\u2082","NH\u2084Cl, N\u2082, NO, HNO\u2083"],c:0,s:`HNO\u2083: 1 + x \u2013 6 = 0, so x = +5.
NO: x \u2013 2 = 0, so x = +2.
N\u2082: x = 0.
NH\u2084Cl (NH\u2084\u207A): x + 4 = +1, so x = \u20133.
Decreasing order: +5, +2, 0, \u20133, that is HNO\u2083, NO, N\u2082, NH\u2084Cl.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Redox Reactions"},{id:"neet-2018-q068",pn:68,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Surface Chemistry",st:"Hardy\u2013Schulze rule",q:"On which of the following properties does the coagulating power of an ion depend ?",o:["The magnitude of the charge on the ion alone","Size of the ion alone","Both magnitude and sign of the charge on the ion","The sign of charge on the ion alone"],c:2,s:`By the Hardy\u2013Schulze rule, the ion carrying a charge opposite to that of the colloid does the coagulating.
Its power grows with the magnitude of that charge.
So it depends on both the sign and the magnitude of the charge.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Surface Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2018-q069",pn:69,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"pH of mixed acid and base",q:"Following solutions were prepared by mixing different volumes of NaOH and HCl of different concentrations : (a) 60 mL (M)/(10) HCl + 40 mL (M)/(10) NaOH (b) 55 mL (M)/(10) HCl + 45 mL (M)/(10) NaOH (c) 75 mL (M)/(5) HCl + 25 mL (M)/(5) NaOH (d) 100 mL (M)/(10) HCl + 100 mL (M)/(10) NaOH. pH of which one of them will be equal to 1 ?",o:["b","a","d","c"],c:3,s:`Find the excess H\u207A after mixing and divide by total volume.
(a) 6 \u2013 4 = 2 meq in 100 mL: 0.02 M, pH = 1.7.
(b) 5.5 \u2013 4.5 = 1 meq in 100 mL: 0.01 M, pH = 2.
(c) 15 \u2013 5 = 10 meq in 100 mL: 0.1 M, pH = 1.
(d) 10 \u2013 10 = 0: neutral, pH = 7.
So (c).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Equilibrium"},{id:"neet-2018-q070",pn:70,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Solubility product",q:"The solubility of BaSO\u2084 in water is 2.42 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 gL\u207B\xB9 at 298 K. The value of its solubility product (Ksp) will be (Given molar mass of BaSO\u2084 = 233 g mol\u207B\xB9)",o:["1.08 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 mol\xB2 L\u207B\xB2","1.08 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 mol\xB2 L\u207B\xB2","1.08 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074 mol\xB2 L\u207B\xB2","1.08 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 mol\xB2 L\u207B\xB2"],c:0,s:`Convert to mol/L: S = (2.42 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)/(233) = 1.04 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 mol/L.
BaSO\u2084 \u21CC Ba\xB2\u207A + SO\u2084\xB2\u207B, so Ksp = S \xD7 S = S\xB2.
Ksp = (1.04 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075)\xB2 = 1.08 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 mol\xB2 L\u207B\xB2.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Equilibrium"},{id:"neet-2018-q071",pn:71,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"States of Matter",st:"van der Waals constant a",q:"Given vanderwaals constant for NH\u2083, H\u2082, O\u2082 and CO\u2082 are respectively 4.17, 0.244, 1.36 and 3.59, which one of the following gases is most easily liquefied ?",o:["NH\u2083","H\u2082","O\u2082","CO\u2082"],c:0,s:`The constant 'a' measures the attraction between molecules.
A larger 'a' means stronger attraction, so the gas liquefies more easily.
NH\u2083 has the largest value (4.17), so it is most easily liquefied.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"States of Matter"},{id:"neet-2018-q072",pn:72,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers",st:"Williamson synthesis",q:"The compound A on treatment with Na gives B, and with PCl\u2085 gives C, B and C react together to give diethyl ether. A, B and C are in the order.",o:["C\u2082H\u2085OH, C\u2082H\u2086, C\u2082H\u2085Cl","C\u2082H\u2085OH, C\u2082H\u2085Cl, C\u2082H\u2085ONa","C\u2082H\u2085Cl, C\u2082H\u2086, C\u2082H\u2085OH","C\u2082H\u2085OH, C\u2082H\u2085ONa, C\u2082H\u2085Cl"],c:3,s:`Diethyl ether comes from sodium ethoxide + ethyl chloride (Williamson synthesis).
A = C\u2082H\u2085OH.
With Na: C\u2082H\u2085OH \u2192 C\u2082H\u2085ONa + \xBDH\u2082, so B = C\u2082H\u2085ONa.
With PCl\u2085: C\u2082H\u2085OH \u2192 C\u2082H\u2085Cl, so C = C\u2082H\u2085Cl.
B + C: C\u2082H\u2085ONa + C\u2082H\u2085Cl \u2192 C\u2082H\u2085OC\u2082H\u2085.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers"},{id:"neet-2018-q073",pn:73,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Wurtz reaction",q:"Hydrocarbon (A) reacts with bromine by substitution to form an alkyl bromide which by Wurtz reaction is converted to gaseous hydrocarbon containing less than four carbon atoms. (A) is",o:["CH\u2261CH","CH\u2082=CH\u2082","CH\u2083\u2013CH\u2083","CH\u2084"],c:3,s:`Alkynes and alkenes mostly add bromine rather than substitute, so (A) is an alkane.
CH\u2084 + Br\u2082 (light) \u2192 CH\u2083Br.
Wurtz: 2CH\u2083Br + 2Na (dry ether) \u2192 CH\u2083\u2013CH\u2083, a gas with 2 carbon atoms (fewer than four).
So (A) = CH\u2084.
Note: Ethane (option 3) would give butane, which has four carbon atoms, so it does not fit 'less than four'; CH\u2084 matches the printed key.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrocarbons"},{id:"neet-2018-q074",pn:74,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Reactions of toluene",q:"The compound C\u2087H\u2088 undergoes the following reactions : C\u2087H\u2088 \u2013(3Cl\u2082/\u0394)\u2192 A \u2013(Br\u2082/Fe)\u2192 B \u2013(Zn/HCl)\u2192 C. The product 'C' is",o:["m-bromotoluene","o-bromotoluene","3-bromo-2,4,6-trichlorotoluene","p-bromotoluene"],c:0,s:`C\u2087H\u2088 is toluene.
3Cl\u2082 with heat or light chlorinates the side chain: A = C\u2086H\u2085CCl\u2083.
\u2013CCl\u2083 is electron-withdrawing and meta directing, so Br\u2082/Fe gives B = m-bromobenzotrichloride.
Zn/HCl reduces \u2013CCl\u2083 back to \u2013CH\u2083.
C = m-bromotoluene.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrocarbons"},{id:"neet-2018-q075",pn:75,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Environmental Chemistry",st:"Oxides of nitrogen as pollutants",q:"Which oxide of nitrogen is not a common pollutant introduced into the atmosphere both due to natural and human activity ?",o:["N\u2082O\u2085","NO\u2082","N\u2082O","NO"],c:0,s:`NO, NO\u2082 and N\u2082O are released both by natural processes (soil bacteria, lightning) and human activity (combustion, fertilisers).
N\u2082O\u2085 has nitrogen in its highest oxidation state, +5, and is not formed in common natural or human processes.
So N\u2082O\u2085 is not a common pollutant.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Environmental Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2018-q076",pn:76,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Redox Reactions",st:"Balancing by ion-electron method",q:"For the redox reaction MnO\u2084\u207B + C\u2082O\u2084\xB2\u207B + H\u207A \u2192 Mn\xB2\u207A + CO\u2082 + H\u2082O the correct coefficients of the reactants for the balanced equation are - (order MnO\u2084\u207B, C\u2082O\u2084\xB2\u207B, H\u207A)",o:["16, 5, 2","2, 5, 16","2, 16, 5","5, 16, 2"],c:1,s:`Oxidation: C\u2082O\u2084\xB2\u207B \u2192 2CO\u2082 + 2e\u207B.
Reduction: MnO\u2084\u207B + 8H\u207A + 5e\u207B \u2192 Mn\xB2\u207A + 4H\u2082O.
Make electrons equal: multiply oxidation by 5 and reduction by 2.
Add: 2MnO\u2084\u207B + 5C\u2082O\u2084\xB2\u207B + 16H\u207A \u2192 2Mn\xB2\u207A + 10CO\u2082 + 8H\u2082O.
Coefficients are 2, 5, 16.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Redox Reactions"},{id:"neet-2018-q077",pn:77,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Le Chatelier's principle",q:"Which one of the following conditions will favour maximum formation of the product in the reaction A\u2082(g) + B\u2082(g) \u21CC X\u2082(g) \u0394rH = \u2013 X kJ ?",o:["Low temperature and high pressure","Low temperature and low pressure","High temperature and high pressure","High temperature and low pressure"],c:0,s:`The reaction is exothermic (\u0394H negative), so low temperature favours the forward reaction.
Moles of gas fall from 2 to 1 (\u0394ng < 0), so high pressure favours the forward reaction.
Best condition: low temperature and high pressure.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Equilibrium"},{id:"neet-2018-q078",pn:78,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"States of Matter",st:"van der Waals equation",q:"The correction factor 'a' to the ideal gas equation corresponds to -",o:["density of the gas molecules","volume of the gas molecules","electric field present between the gas molecules","forces of attraction between the gas molecules"],c:3,s:`In (P + (an\xB2)/(V\xB2))(V \u2013 nb) = nRT, the term an\xB2/V\xB2 corrects the pressure for attraction between molecules.
(The constant b corrects for molecular volume.)
So 'a' corresponds to forces of attraction.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"States of Matter"},{id:"neet-2018-q079",pn:79,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Half-life of zero order reaction",q:"When initial concentration of the reactant is doubled, the half-life period of a zero order reaction",o:["is halved","is doubled","is tripled","remains unchanged"],c:1,s:`For zero order, t\xBD = ([A]\u2080)/(2k).
So t\xBD is directly proportional to [A]\u2080.
Doubling [A]\u2080 doubles the half-life.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Kinetics"},{id:"neet-2018-q080",pn:80,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)",st:"Enthalpy from bond energies",q:"The bond dissociation energies of X\u2082, Y\u2082 and XY are in the ratio of 1 : 0.5 : 1. \u0394H for the formation of XY is \u2013200 kJ mol\u207B\xB9. The bond dissociation energy of X\u2082 will be -",o:["200 kJ mol\u207B\xB9","100 kJ mol\u207B\xB9","800 kJ mol\u207B\xB9","400 kJ mol\u207B\xB9"],c:2,s:`Reaction: \xBDX\u2082 + \xBDY\u2082 \u2192 XY.
Let the bond energies be X\u2082 = a, Y\u2082 = 0.5a, XY = a.
\u0394H = (energy of bonds broken) \u2013 (energy of bonds formed) = (a/2 + 0.5a/2) \u2013 a = 0.75a \u2013 a = \u20130.25a.
\u20130.25a = \u2013200, so a = 800 kJ mol\u207B\xB9.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2018-q081",pn:81,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers",st:"Cumene process",q:"Identify the major products P, Q and R in the following sequence of reactions - benzene + CH\u2083CH\u2082CH\u2082Cl (anhydrous AlCl\u2083) \u2192 R, then R (i) O\u2082 (ii) H\u2083O\u207A/\u0394 \u2192 Q + R",o:["P = n-propylbenzene, Q = benzaldehyde, R = CH\u2083CH\u2082\u2013OH","P = n-propylbenzene, Q = benzaldehyde, R = benzoic acid","P = cumene (CH(CH\u2083)\u2082 on ring), Q = phenol, R = CH\u2083CH(OH)CH\u2083","P = cumene (CH(CH\u2083)\u2082 on ring), Q = phenol, R = CH\u2083\u2013CO\u2013CH\u2083"],c:3,s:`Friedel\u2013Crafts alkylation: the secondary carbocation formed from n-propyl chloride rearranges, so the product is cumene (isopropylbenzene), not n-propylbenzene.
Cumene is oxidised by O\u2082 to cumene hydroperoxide.
Acid hydrolysis of the hydroperoxide gives phenol and acetone.
So the products are cumene, phenol and acetone.
Note: The question's arrow is labelled R though the first product is P, as printed.`,fig:{w:420,h:440,alt:"Benzene with CH3CH2CH2Cl and anhydrous AlCl3 gives P; P with O2 then H3O+ and heat gives Q + R. Options (1) to (4) give the structures of P, Q and R.",d:[["p","M45 26L58.9 34L58.9 50L45 58L31.1 50L31.1 34Z",""],["c",45,42,9.3,""],["t",76,46,"+ CH\u2083CH\u2082CH\u2082Cl",""],["l",206,42,258,42,"a"],["t",232,14,"Anhydrous","ms"],["t",232,27,"AlCl\u2083","ms"],["t",266,46,"R",""],["l",288,42,376,42,"a"],["t",332,34,"(i) O\u2082","ms"],["t",332,58,"(ii) H\u2083O\u207A/\u0394","ms"],["t",386,46,"Q + R",""],["t",50,88,"P","m"],["t",178,88,"Q","m"],["t",281,88,"R","m"],["t",3,159,"(1)",""],["p","M48 139L61.9 147L61.9 163L48 171L34.1 163L34.1 147Z",""],["c",48,155,9.3,""],["l",48,139,48,128,""],["t",48,124,"CH\u2082CH\u2082CH\u2083","m"],["t",94,171,",",""],["p","M178 139L191.9 147L191.9 163L178 171L164.1 163L164.1 147Z",""],["c",178,155,9.3,""],["l",178,139,178,128,""],["t",178,124,"CHO","m"],["t",222,171,",",""],["t",258,159,"CH\u2083CH\u2082 \u2013 OH",""],["t",3,244,"(2)",""],["p","M52 224L65.9 232L65.9 248L52 256L38.1 248L38.1 232Z",""],["c",52,240,9.3,""],["l",52,224,52,213,""],["t",52,209,"CH\u2082CH\u2082CH\u2083","m"],["t",96,256,",",""],["p","M178 224L191.9 232L191.9 248L178 256L164.1 248L164.1 232Z",""],["c",178,240,9.3,""],["l",178,224,178,213,""],["t",178,209,"CHO","m"],["t",222,256,",",""],["p","M282 224L295.9 232L295.9 248L282 256L268.1 248L268.1 232Z",""],["c",282,240,9.3,""],["l",282,224,282,213,""],["t",282,209,"COOH","m"],["t",3,329,"(3)",""],["p","M50 309L63.9 317L63.9 333L50 341L36.1 333L36.1 317Z",""],["c",50,325,9.3,""],["l",63.9,317,77.9,313,""],["t",79.9,315,"CH(CH\u2083)\u2082",""],["t",94,341,",",""],["p","M182 309L195.9 317L195.9 333L182 341L168.1 333L168.1 317Z",""],["c",182,325,9.3,""],["l",195.9,317,205.9,307,""],["t",207.9,305,"OH",""],["t",222,341,",",""],["t",264,329,"CH\u2083CH(OH)CH\u2083",""],["t",3,414,"(4)",""],["p","M55 394L68.9 402L68.9 418L55 426L41.1 418L41.1 402Z",""],["c",55,410,9.3,""],["l",68.9,402,82.9,398,""],["t",84.9,400,"CH(CH\u2083)\u2082",""],["t",98,426,",",""],["p","M190 394L203.9 402L203.9 418L190 426L176.1 418L176.1 402Z",""],["c",190,410,9.3,""],["l",190,394,190,383,""],["t",190,379,"OH","m"],["t",226,426,",",""],["t",262,414,"CH\u2083\u2013CO\u2013CH\u2083",""]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers"},{id:"neet-2018-q082",pn:82,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Zwitterion of amino acids",q:"Which of the following compounds can form a zwitterion ?",o:["Aniline","Acetanilide","Benzoic acid","Glycine"],c:3,s:`A zwitterion needs both an acidic group (\u2013COOH) and a basic group (\u2013NH\u2082) in one molecule.
Glycine has both: NH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013COOH changes to \u207ANH\u2083\u2013CH\u2082\u2013COO\u207B by internal proton transfer.
Aniline, acetanilide and benzoic acid have only one type of group.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2018-q083",pn:83,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Coordination Compounds",st:"Isomerism in coordination compounds",q:"The type of isomerism shown by the complex [CoCl\u2082(en)\u2082] is -",o:["Geometrical isomerism","Coordination isomerism","Ionization isomerism","Linkage isomerism"],c:0,s:`The complex is octahedral of type [M(AA)\u2082X\u2082] with en as a bidentate ligand.
The two Cl\u207B can be next to each other (cis) or opposite (trans).
So it shows geometrical isomerism (cis and trans forms).`,sfig:{w:330,h:165,alt:"cis and trans forms of Co with two en ligands and two Cl; in cis the Cl are adjacent, in trans they are opposite.",d:[["r",32,49,78,48,""],["l",32,49,57,64,""],["l",32,97,57,82,""],["l",110,49,85,64,""],["l",110,97,85,82,""],["t",71,78,"Co","ms"],["l",71,49,71,17,""],["t",71,12,"Cl","m"],["l",110,49,119,40,""],["t",121,42,"Cl",""],["t",3,78,"en",""],["p","M32 49C19 55 19 91 32 97",""],["p","M108 97V108Q108 113 100 113",""],["t",97,126,"en",""],["t",71,152,"cis","m"],["r",203,49,80,48,""],["l",203,49,228,64,""],["l",203,97,228,82,""],["l",283,49,258,64,""],["l",283,97,258,82,""],["t",243,78,"Co","ms"],["l",243,49,243,17,""],["t",243,12,"Cl","m"],["l",243,97,243,121,""],["t",243,134,"Cl","m"],["p","M203 49C190 55 190 91 203 97",""],["p","M283 49C296 55 296 91 283 97",""],["t",187,78,"en","e"],["t",301,78,"en",""],["t",243,152,"trans","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Coordination Compounds"},{id:"neet-2018-q084",pn:84,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"d-d transitions and magnetism",q:"Which one of the following ions exhibits d-d transition and paramagnetism as well ?",o:["CrO\u2084\xB2\u207B","Cr\u2082O\u2087\xB2\u207B","MnO\u2084\u207B","MnO\u2084\xB2\u207B"],c:3,s:`d-d transitions and paramagnetism both need partly filled d orbitals, not d\u2070 or d\xB9\u2070.
CrO\u2084\xB2\u207B and Cr\u2082O\u2087\xB2\u207B: Cr is +6, d\u2070. MnO\u2084\u207B: Mn is +7, d\u2070.
MnO\u2084\xB2\u207B: Mn is +6, [Ar]3d\xB9, one unpaired electron.
So only MnO\u2084\xB2\u207B is paramagnetic and shows d-d transition.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The d- and f-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2018-q085",pn:85,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Coordination Compounds",st:"Metal carbonyls",q:"The geometry and magnetic behavior of the complex [Ni(CO)\u2084] are",o:["square planar geometry and diamagnetic","tetrahedral geometry and diamagnetic","square planar geometry and paramagnetic","tetrahedral geometry and paramagnetic"],c:1,s:`Ni is 3d\u20784s\xB2 and CO is a strong-field ligand, so the 4s electrons move into 3d, giving 3d\xB9\u2070.
All electrons are paired, so it is diamagnetic.
Ni uses one 4s and three 4p orbitals (sp\xB3), so the shape is tetrahedral.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Coordination Compounds"},{id:"neet-2018-q086",pn:86,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Coordination Compounds",st:"Metal carbonyls",q:"Iron carbonyl, Fe(CO)\u2085 is",o:["tetranuclear","mononuclear","trinuclear","dinuclear"],c:1,s:`Fe(CO)\u2085 has only one metal atom (Fe), so it is mononuclear.
Its shape is trigonal bipyramidal.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Coordination Compounds"},{id:"neet-2018-q087",pn:87,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Coordination Compounds",st:"Spin-only magnetic moment",q:"Match the metal ions given in column I with the spin magnetic moments of the ions given in Column II and assign the correct code - Column I: (a) Co\xB3\u207A (b) Cr\xB3\u207A (c) Fe\xB3\u207A (d) Ni\xB2\u207A. Column II: (i) \u221A8 B.M. (ii) \u221A35 B.M. (iii) \u221A3 B.M. (iv) \u221A24 B.M. (v) \u221A15 B.M.",o:["a-iv, b-v, c-ii, d-i","a-i, b-ii, c-iii, d-iv","a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii","a-iii, b-v, c-i, d-ii"],c:0,s:`Spin-only moment \u03BC = \u221A(n(n+2)) B.M., n = number of unpaired electrons.
Co\xB3\u207A is 3d\u2076 (high spin), n = 4, \u03BC = \u221A24.
Cr\xB3\u207A is 3d\xB3, n = 3, \u03BC = \u221A15.
Fe\xB3\u207A is 3d\u2075, n = 5, \u03BC = \u221A35.
Ni\xB2\u207A is 3d\u2078, n = 2, \u03BC = \u221A8.
Match: a\u2013iv, b\u2013v, c\u2013ii, d\u2013i.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Coordination Compounds"},{id:"neet-2018-q088",pn:88,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques",st:"Inductive effect",q:"Which of the following is correct with respect to \u2013I effect of the substituents ? (R = alkyl)",o:["\u2013NH\u2082 < \u2013OR < \u2013F","\u2013NR\u2082 < \u2013OR < \u2013F","\u2013NH\u2082 > \u2013OR < \u2013F","\u2013NR\u2082 > \u2013OR > \u2013F"],a:"1 and 2",s:`The \u2013I effect grows with the electronegativity of the atom attached to the carbon.
Electronegativity: N < O < F.
So \u2013NH\u2082 < \u2013OR < \u2013F, and \u2013NR\u2082 < \u2013OR < \u2013F also follows.
Both options (1) and (2) are therefore correct.
Note: Printed answer is '1 and 2' (two options); no single index.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques"},{id:"neet-2018-q089",pn:89,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques",st:"Carbocation stability",q:"Which of the following carbocations is expected to be most stable ?",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:2,s:`These are arenium ions with Y and H on the sp\xB3 carbon and NO\u2082 on the ring.
\u2013NO\u2082 is strongly electron-withdrawing, so it destabilises the positive charge.
The ion is most stable when the positive charge is delocalised away from the carbon holding NO\u2082, which is the case in option (3).
Note: The exact ring positions of NO\u2082 and the charge are only described approximately; check the printed figure before redrawing.`,fig:{w:340,h:245,alt:"Four cyclohexadienyl cations (1) to (4), each with NO2 on the ring, H and Y on the sp3 carbon, and a positive charge at different ring positions.",d:[["l",61.2,35.7,79.3,45.6,""],["l",79.3,45.6,79.3,67.1,""],["l",79.3,67.1,61.2,77.7,""],["l",61.2,77.7,42.3,67.1,""],["l",42.3,67.1,42.3,45.6,""],["l",42.3,45.6,61.2,35.7,""],["l",62.2,39.9,75.2,47.1,""],["l",45.5,64.1,45.5,48.6,""],["t",12.6,61.9,"(1)","m"],["t",61.2,23.9,"NO\u2082","m"],["l",61.2,35.7,61.2,25.8,""],["l",61.2,77.7,44.7,89.2,""],["l",61.2,77.7,76.7,89.2,""],["t",42,99.1,"Y","m"],["t",78.6,99.1,"H","m"],["c",86.6,67.1,5,""],["l",83.6,67.1,89.6,67.1,""],["l",86.6,64.1,86.6,70.1,""],["l",225.2,35.7,243.7,45.6,""],["l",243.7,45.6,243.7,67.1,""],["l",243.7,67.1,225.2,77.7,""],["l",225.2,77.7,206.4,67.1,""],["l",206.4,67.1,206.4,45.6,""],["l",206.4,45.6,225.2,35.7,""],["l",240.5,48.6,240.5,64.1,""],["l",209.6,64.1,209.6,48.6,""],["t",181,61.9,"(2)","m"],["t",225.2,23.9,"NO\u2082","m"],["l",225.2,35.7,225.2,25.8,""],["l",225.2,77.7,208.7,89.2,""],["l",225.2,77.7,240.1,89.2,""],["t",206.4,99.1,"Y","m"],["t",242.7,99.1,"H","m"],["c",225.2,43,5,""],["l",222.2,43,228.2,43,""],["l",225.2,40,225.2,46,""],["l",83.6,154,102.4,163.9,""],["l",102.4,163.9,102.4,185.4,""],["l",102.4,185.4,83.6,196,""],["l",83.6,196,65.4,185.4,""],["l",65.4,185.4,65.4,163.9,""],["l",65.4,163.9,83.6,154,""],["l",69.5,165.4,82.6,158.2,""],["l",99.2,166.9,99.2,182.4,""],["t",12.6,170.3,"(3)","m"],["t",94.2,140.6,"NO\u2082","m"],["l",83.6,154,83.6,144.1,""],["l",65.4,185.4,47,185.4,""],["l",65.4,185.4,51.6,196,""],["t",40.4,189.4,"H","m"],["t",47,205.6,"Y","m"],["c",84.3,203.5,5,""],["l",81.3,203.5,87.3,203.5,""],["l",84.3,200.5,84.3,206.5,""],["l",247.7,151.4,266.5,162,""],["l",266.5,162,266.5,182.8,""],["l",266.5,182.8,247.7,193.6,""],["l",247.7,193.6,228.8,182.8,""],["l",228.8,182.8,228.8,162,""],["l",228.8,162,247.7,151.4,""],["l",263.3,164.9,263.3,179.8,""],["l",246.6,189.3,233.1,181.5,""],["t",185,153.8,"(4)","m"],["t",258.2,135.6,"NO\u2082","m"],["l",247.7,151.4,247.7,141.5,""],["l",228.8,162,213,153.1,""],["l",228.8,182.8,228.8,162,""],["t",205.4,153.8,"H","m"],["l",228.8,162,219.6,168.9,""],["t",207.4,179.2,"Y","m"],["c",247.7,159,5,""],["l",244.7,159,250.7,159,""],["l",247.7,156,247.7,162,""]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques"},{id:"neet-2018-q090",pn:90,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques",st:"Hybridisation",q:"Which of the following molecules represents the order of hybridisation sp\xB2, sp\xB2, sp, sp from left to right atoms ?",o:["HC\u2261C\u2013C\u2261CH","CH\u2082=CH\u2013C\u2261CH","CH\u2082=CH\u2013CH=CH\u2082","CH\u2083\u2013CH=CH\u2013CH\u2083"],c:1,s:`A carbon in a double bond is sp\xB2, a carbon in a triple bond is sp.
CH\u2082=CH\u2013C\u2261CH: C1 and C2 are in a double bond (sp\xB2, sp\xB2), C3 and C4 in a triple bond (sp, sp).
So the order is sp\xB2, sp\xB2, sp, sp.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques"},{id:"neet-2018-q091",pn:91,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Meselson and Stahl experiment",q:"The experimental proof for semiconservative replication of DNA was first shown in a -",o:["Fungus","Bacterium","Plant","Virus"],c:1,s:`Meselson and Stahl grew E. coli (a bacterium) in \xB9\u2075N medium and then moved it to \xB9\u2074N medium.
They separated the DNA by density gradient centrifugation and showed that new DNA has one old and one new strand.
So the proof was first shown in a bacterium.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2018-q092",pn:92,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"History of genetics",q:"Select the correct statement -",o:["Franklin Stahl coined the term \u201Clinkage\u201D","Punnett square was developed by a British scientist.","Spliceosomes take part in traslation","Transduction was discovered by S.Altman"],c:1,s:`Reginald C. Punnett, a British geneticist, developed the Punnett square, so (2) is correct.
The term linkage was coined by T.H. Morgan, not Stahl.
Spliceosomes work in splicing of hnRNA (processing), not translation.
Transduction was discovered by Zinder and Lederberg; S. Altman worked on ribozymes.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2018-q093",pn:93,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Vegetative propagation",q:"Offsets are produced by",o:["Meiotic divisions","Mitotic divisions","Parthenocarpy","Parthenogenesis"],c:1,s:`An offset is a short side branch of the stem (as in water hyacinth) that grows into a new plant.
It is a form of vegetative (asexual) reproduction.
Asexual reproduction involves only mitosis, so offsets form by mitotic divisions.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2018-q094",pn:94,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Genetic concepts",q:"Which of the following pairs is wrongly matched ?",o:["Starch synthesis in pea : Multiple alleles","ABO blood grouping : Co-dominance","XO type sex determination : Grasshopper","T.H.Morgan : Linkage"],c:0,s:`Starch synthesis in pea is controlled by one gene with two alleles whose effect on starch grain size and seed shape is pleiotropy, not multiple alleles.
ABO groups show co-dominance (IA and IB), grasshopper has XO sex determination, and Morgan studied linkage.
So pair (1) is wrong.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2018-q095",pn:95,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Flowering patterns",q:"Which of the following flowers only once in its life time ?",o:["Bamboo species","Jackfruit","Mango","Papaya"],c:0,s:`Bamboo is monocarpic: it flowers once after many years and then dies.
Jackfruit, mango and papaya are polycarpic and flower every year.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2018-q096",pn:96,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Scientists and experiments",q:"Select the correct match :",o:["Alec jeffreys : Streptococcus pneumoniae","Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase : TMV","Matthew Meselson and F.Stahl : Pisum sativum","Francois Jacob and Jacques Monod : Lac operon"],c:3,s:`Jacob and Monod proposed the lac operon model in E. coli.
Alec Jeffreys is linked with DNA fingerprinting; Streptococcus pneumoniae goes with Griffith.
Hershey and Chase used bacteriophage, and Meselson and Stahl used E. coli.
So only option (4) is correct.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2018-q097",pn:97,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Pollen wall",q:"Which of the following has proved helpful in preserving pollen as fossils ?",o:["Pollenkitt","Cellulosic intine","Oil content","Sporopollenin"],c:3,s:`The outer wall of pollen (exine) is made of sporopollenin.
Sporopollenin resists high temperature, strong acids and alkalis and no enzyme is known to degrade it.
So it preserves pollen as fossils.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2018-q098",pn:98,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Transport in Plants",st:"Stomatal movement",q:"Stomatal movement is not affected by -",o:["Temperature","Light","O\u2082 concentration","CO\u2082 concentration"],c:2,s:`Guard cells respond to light, temperature, CO\u2082 concentration and water status.
O\u2082 concentration has no direct effect on stomatal opening.
So the answer is O\u2082 concentration.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Transport in Plants"},{id:"neet-2018-q099",pn:99,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Meiotic prophase I",q:"The stage during which separation of the paired homologous chromosomes begins is",o:["Pachytene","Diplotene","Diakinesis","Zygotene"],c:1,s:`In diplotene the synaptonemal complex dissolves.
The homologous chromosomes begin to separate, except at the chiasmata where crossing over took place.
So separation begins in diplotene.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2018-q100",pn:100,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Functional groups of sugars",q:"The two functional groups characteristic of sugars are -",o:["Hydroxyl and methyl","Carbonyl and methyl","Carbonyl and phosphate","Carbonyl and hydroxyl"],c:3,s:`Sugars are polyhydroxy aldehydes or ketones.
So each sugar has a carbonyl group (aldehyde or ketone) and several hydroxyl groups.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biomolecules (Biology)"},{id:"neet-2018-q101",pn:101,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants",st:"Products of light reaction",q:"Which of the following is not a product of light reaction of photosynthesis ?",o:["ATP","NADH","NADPH","Oxygen"],c:1,s:`The light reaction makes ATP (photophosphorylation), NADPH (reduction of NADP\u207A) and O\u2082 (photolysis of water).
NADH is made in respiration, not in photosynthesis.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants"},{id:"neet-2018-q102",pn:102,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Stomata of grasses",q:"Stomata in grass leaf are -",o:["Dumb-bell shaped","Kidney shaped","Rectangular","Barrel shaped"],c:0,s:`Grasses are monocots.
Their guard cells are dumb-bell shaped, while dicots have kidney-shaped guard cells.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2018-q103",pn:103,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Prokaryotes and eukaryotes",q:"Which among the following is not a prokaryote ?",o:["Saccharomyces","Mycobacterium","Nostoc","Oscillatoria"],c:0,s:`Saccharomyces is yeast, a unicellular fungus, so it is eukaryotic.
Mycobacterium is a bacterium, and Nostoc and Oscillatoria are cyanobacteria; all are prokaryotes.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2018-q104",pn:104,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Nucleolus",q:"Which of the following is true for nucleolus ?",o:["Larger nucleoli are present in dividing cells","It is a membrane-bound structure","It takes part in spindle formation","It is a site for active ribosomal RNA synthesis."],c:3,s:`The nucleolus is a non-membrane-bound region in the nucleus where rRNA is made and ribosome subunits are assembled.
It is large in cells making lots of protein, and it disappears during division.
So only (4) is true.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2018-q105",pn:105,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Golgi apparatus",q:"The Golgi complex participates in -",o:["Fatty acid breakdown","Formation of secretory vesicles","Respiration in bacteria","Activation of amino acid"],c:1,s:`The Golgi apparatus modifies, sorts and packages materials from the ER.
It forms secretory vesicles.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2018-q106",pn:106,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Environmental Chemistry",st:"Ozone depletion",q:"In stratosphere, which of the following elements acts as a catalyst in degradation of ozone and release of molecular oxygen ?",o:["Carbon","Cl","Fe","Oxygen"],c:1,s:`UV rays break CFCs in the stratosphere and free chlorine atoms (Cl).
Cl + O\u2083 \u2192 ClO + O\u2082, then ClO + O \u2192 Cl + O\u2082.
Cl is regenerated, so it acts as a catalyst.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Environmental Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2018-q107",pn:107,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Environmental Chemistry",st:"Primary and secondary pollutants",q:"Which of the following is a secondary pollutant ?",o:["CO","CO\u2082","SO\u2082","O\u2083"],c:3,s:`Primary pollutants (CO, CO\u2082, SO\u2082) are emitted directly.
Secondary pollutants form by reactions among primary pollutants.
Ground-level O\u2083 forms from NO\u2093 and hydrocarbons in sunlight, so it is secondary.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Environmental Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2018-q108",pn:108,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Niche",q:"Niche is -",o:["All the biological factors in the organism's environment","The physical space where an organism lives","The range of temperature that the organism needs to live","The functional role played by the organism where it lives."],c:3,s:`Habitat is the place where an organism lives.
Niche is the functional role of an organism in its habitat, including its resources and interactions.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Organisms and Populations"},{id:"neet-2018-q109",pn:109,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population attributes",q:"Natality refers to",o:["Death rate","Birth rate","Number of individuals leaving the habitat","Number of individuals entering a habitat"],c:1,s:`Natality is the number of births in a population in a given time, so it is the birth rate.
Mortality is death rate, emigration is leaving and immigration is entering.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Organisms and Populations"},{id:"neet-2018-q110",pn:110,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ecological pyramids",q:"What type of ecological pyramid would be obtained with the following data ? Secondary consumer : 120 g, Primary consumer : 60 g, Primary producer : 10 g",o:["Inverted pyramid of biomass","Pyramid of energy","Upright pyramid of numbers","Upright pyramid of biomass"],c:0,s:`The data are in grams, so they are biomass.
Producers have the smallest biomass (10 g) and the top consumers the largest (120 g), so the pyramid is inverted.
Such pyramids occur in aquatic ecosystems.`,sfig:{w:220,h:108,alt:"Inverted pyramid of biomass: 120 g at the top, 60 g in the middle, 10 g at the bottom.",d:[["r",55,10,112,28,""],["r",75,38,72,28,""],["r",93,66,36,30,""],["t",111,29,"120 g","m"],["t",111,57,"60 g","m"],["t",111,86,"10 g","m"]]},sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2018-q111",pn:111,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Environmental Chemistry",st:"Ozone day",q:"World Ozone Day is celebrated on",o:["5th June","21st April","16th September","22nd April"],c:2,s:`The Montreal Protocol was signed on 16 September 1987.
So World Ozone Day is 16 September.
(5 June is World Environment Day and 22 April is Earth Day.)`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Environmental Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2018-q112",pn:112,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Vectors",q:"Which of the following is commonly used as a vector for introducing a DNA fragment in human lymphocytes ?",o:["Retrovirus","Ti plasmid","\u03BB phage","pBR 322"],c:0,s:`Retroviruses can enter animal cells and integrate their genes into the host genome.
Disarmed retroviruses are used to carry genes into human lymphocytes (for example in gene therapy for ADA deficiency).
Ti plasmid is for plants, and \u03BB phage and pBR322 are used in bacteria.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2018-q113",pn:113,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biotechnology and its Applications",st:"Regulation of GM organisms",q:"In India, the organisation responsible for assessing the safety of introducing genetically modified organisms for public use is -",o:["Indian Council of Medical Research (ICMR)","Council for Scientific and Industrial Research (CSIR)","Research Committee on Genetic Manipulation (RCGM)","Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee (GEAC)"],c:3,s:`The Government of India set up the Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee (GEAC).
It decides whether GM research is valid and whether GM organisms are safe for public use.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology and its Applications"},{id:"neet-2018-q114",pn:114,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biotechnology and its Applications",st:"Biopiracy and patents",q:"A 'new' variety of rice was patented by a foreign company, though such varieties have been present in India for a long time. This is related to -",o:["Co-667","Sharbati Sonora","Lerma Rojo","Basmati"],c:3,s:`A US company got a patent on Basmati rice lines derived from Indian varieties.
Co-667 is sugarcane, and Sharbati Sonora and Lerma Rojo are wheat varieties.
So it is Basmati.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology and its Applications"},{id:"neet-2018-q115",pn:115,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Genetics terms and scientists",q:"Select the correct match :",o:["Ribozyme - Nucleic acid","F\u2082 \xD7 Recessive parent - Dihybrid cross","T.H.Morgan - Transduction","G.Mendel - Transformation"],c:0,s:`A ribozyme is a catalytic RNA, that is a nucleic acid enzyme, so (1) is correct.
F\u2082 \xD7 recessive parent is not a dihybrid cross; a cross of F\u2081 with the recessive parent is a test cross.
Morgan worked on linkage; transduction is Zinder and Lederberg.
Transformation is Griffith, not Mendel.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2018-q116",pn:116,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biotechnology and its Applications",st:"Biopiracy",q:"Use of bioresources by multinational companies and organisations without authorisation from the concerned country and its people is called -",o:["Bio-infringement","Biopiracy","Biodegradation","Bioexploitation"],c:1,s:`Using bioresources or traditional knowledge without proper authorisation and compensation is called biopiracy.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology and its Applications"},{id:"neet-2018-q117",pn:117,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Steps of PCR",q:"The correct order of steps in Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) is -",o:["Extension, Denaturation, Annealing","Annealing, Extension, Denaturation","Denaturation, Extension, Annealing","Denaturation, Annealing, Extension"],c:3,s:`Denaturation: heat separates the two strands of the DNA.
Annealing: primers bind to the single strands.
Extension: Taq polymerase adds nucleotides to make new strands.
So the order is denaturation, annealing, extension.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2018-q118",pn:118,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Secondary growth",q:"Secondary xylem and phloem in dicot stem are produced by",o:["Apical meristems","Vascular cambium","Phellogen","Axillary meristems"],c:1,s:`Vascular cambium divides to cut off secondary xylem inwards and secondary phloem outwards.
Phellogen makes cork, and apical meristems give primary growth.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2018-q119",pn:119,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Root modifications",q:"Pneumatophores occur in",o:["Halophytes","Free-floating hydrophytes","Carnivorous plants","Submerged hydrophytes"],c:0,s:`Pneumatophores are upward-growing roots with pores that take in air.
They occur in halophytes (mangroves) growing in marshy, oxygen-poor soil.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2018-q120",pn:120,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Root modifications",q:"Sweet potato is a modified",o:["Stem","Adventitious root","Tap root","Rhizome"],c:1,s:`Sweet potato (Ipomoea batatas) is a tuberous root that grows from the stem nodes, so it is an adventitious root.
Potato, in contrast, is a stem tuber.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2018-q121",pn:121,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Gymnosperms and pteridophytes",q:"Which of the following statements is correct ?",o:["Ovules are not enclosed by ovary wall in gymnosperms.","Selaginella is heterosporous, while salvinia is homosporous.","Horsetails are gymnosperms.","Stems are usually unbranched in both Cycas and Cedrus."],c:0,s:`(1) Gymnosperm ovules are naked, so this is correct.
(2) Both Selaginella and Salvinia are heterosporous.
(3) Horsetails (Equisetum) are pteridophytes.
(4) Cycas stem is unbranched but Cedrus is branched.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2018-q122",pn:122,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Five kingdoms",q:"Select the wrong statement :",o:["Cell wall is present in members of Fungi and Plantae.","Mushrooms belong to Basidiomycetes.","Pseudopodia are locomotory and feeding structures in Sporozoans.","Mitochondria are the powerhouse of the cell in all kingdoms excepts Monera."],c:2,s:`Cell walls occur in fungi and plants (1 true); mushrooms are basidiomycetes (2 true); Monera has no mitochondria (4 true).
Pseudopodia are used by amoeboid protozoans (sarcodines).
Sporozoans have no locomotory structures, so (3) is wrong.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2018-q123",pn:123,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Casparian strip",q:"Casparian strips occur in -",o:["Epidermis","Pericycle","Cortex","Endoermis"],c:3,s:`Casparian strips are bands of suberin on the radial and inner tangential walls of endodermal cells in roots.
They block the apoplast pathway of water.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2018-q124",pn:124,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Secondary growth",q:"Plants having little or no secondary growth are -",o:["Grasses","Deciduous angiosperms","Cenifers","Cycads"],c:0,s:`Grasses are monocots with closed vascular bundles (no cambium), so they show little or no secondary growth.
Deciduous angiosperms, conifers and cycads do show secondary growth.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2018-q125",pn:125,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Algae and bryophytes",q:"Which one is wrongly matched ?",o:["Uniflagellate gametes \u2013 Polysiphonia","Biflagellate zoospores \u2013 Brown algae","Gemma cups \u2013 Marchantia","Unicellular organism \u2013 Chlorella"],c:0,s:`Polysiphonia is a red alga and has no flagellated stage at all.
Brown algae make biflagellate zoospores, Marchantia has gemma cups and Chlorella is unicellular.
So (1) is wrongly matched.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2018-q126",pn:126,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"The Living World",st:"Taxonomic aids",q:"Match the items given in Column I with those in column II. Column-I: a. Herbarium, b. Key, c. Museum, d. Catalogue. Column-II: i. A place having a collection of preserved plants and animals. ii. A list that enumerates methodically all the species found in an area with brief description aiding identification. iii. A place where dried and pressed plants specimens mounted on sheets are kept. iv. A booklet containing a list of characters and their alternates which are helpful in identification of various taxa.",o:["a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii","a-iii, b-ii, c-i, d-iv","a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i","a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii"],c:3,s:`Herbarium: dried, pressed plants on sheets, so a\u2013iii.
Key: booklet of contrasting characters used for identification, so b\u2013iv.
Museum: preserved plants and animals, so c\u2013i.
Catalogue: methodical list of species with brief descriptions, so d\u2013ii.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"The Living World"},{id:"neet-2018-q127",pn:127,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Gymnosperm pollen",q:"Winged pollen grains are present in -",o:["Mustard","Cycas","Mango","Pinus"],c:3,s:`Pinus has pollen grains with two air-filled wings that help wind pollination.
Mustard, Cycas and Mango do not have winged pollen.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2018-q128",pn:128,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Basidiomycetes",q:"After karyogamy followed by meiosis, spores are produced exogenously in -",o:["Neurospora","Alternaria","Agaricus","Saccharomyces"],c:2,s:`Basidiomycetes such as Agaricus make basidiospores exogenously on the basidium after karyogamy and meiosis.
Neurospora and Saccharomyces form ascospores inside an ascus (endogenously), and Alternaria has no sexual stage.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2018-q129",pn:129,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Role of NAD\u207A",q:"What is the role of NAD\u207A in cellular respiration ?",o:["It functions as an enzyme","It functions as an electron carrier.","It is nucleotide source for ATP synthesis","It is the final electron acceptor for anaerobic respiration"],c:1,s:`NAD\u207A accepts electrons and protons (2e\u207B and 2H\u207A) and becomes NADH.
NADH carries them to the electron transport chain.
So NAD\u207A works as an electron carrier, not as an enzyme.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Respiration in Plants"},{id:"neet-2018-q130",pn:130,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants",st:"Oxygenic and anoxygenic photosynthesis",q:"Oxygen is not produced during photosynthesis by -",o:["Green sulphur bacteria","Nostoc","Cycas","Chara"],c:0,s:`Cyanobacteria (Nostoc), algae (Chara) and plants (Cycas) split water and release O\u2082.
Green sulphur bacteria use H\u2082S instead of water, so they do not release O\u2082.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants"},{id:"neet-2018-q131",pn:131,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Pollen storage",q:"Pollen grains can be stored for several years in liquid nitrogen having a temperature of -",o:["\u2013120\xBAC","\u201380\xBAC","\u2013196\xBAC","\u2013160\xBAC"],c:2,s:`Liquid nitrogen has a boiling point of \u2013196 \xBAC.
Pollen stored at this temperature (cryopreservation) stays viable for years in pollen banks.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2018-q132",pn:132,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Mineral Nutrition",st:"Iron uptake",q:"In which of the following forms is iron absorbed by plants -",o:["Ferric","Ferrous","Free element","Both ferric and ferrous"],c:0,s:`Plants take up iron as ferric ions (Fe\xB3\u207A).
Note: Printed answer 1 (ferric). Many texts say plants absorb Fe\xB2\u207A as well; printed key kept.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Mineral Nutrition"},{id:"neet-2018-q133",pn:133,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Double fertilisation",q:"Double fertilization is -",o:["Fusion of two male gametes of a pollen tube with two different eggs.","Fusion of one male gamete with two polar nuclei","Fusion of two male gametes with one egg","Syngamy and triple fusion"],c:3,s:`One male gamete fuses with the egg: syngamy, giving the zygote.
The other fuses with the two polar nuclei: triple fusion, giving the primary endosperm nucleus.
Together these are double fertilisation.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2018-q134",pn:134,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Mineral Nutrition",st:"Role of potassium",q:"Which of the following elements is responsible for maintaining turgor in cells ?",o:["Magnesium","Sodium","Potassium","Calcium"],c:2,s:`Potassium helps maintain the osmotic pressure of cells and the turgidity of guard cells.
So potassium maintains turgor.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Mineral Nutrition"},{id:"neet-2018-q135",pn:135,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Mutualism",q:"Which one of the following plants shows a very close relationship with a species of moth, where none of the two can complete its life cycle without the other ?",o:["Hydrilla","Yucca","Banana","Viola"],c:1,s:`Yucca and the Pronuba moth are mutualists.
The moth pollinates the flower and lays eggs in the ovary, and the larvae feed on some seeds.
Neither can finish its life cycle without the other.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Organisms and Populations"},{id:"neet-2018-q136",pn:136,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Placental hormones",q:"Hormones secreted by the placenta to maintain pregnancy are -",o:["hCG, hPL, progestogens, prolactin","hCG, hPL, estrogens, relaxin, oxytocin","hCG, hPL, progestogens, estrogens","hCG, progestogens, estrogens, glucocorticoids"],c:2,s:`The placenta secretes hCG, hPL, estrogens and progestogens to maintain pregnancy.
Relaxin is made in the later part of pregnancy by the ovary, and prolactin and oxytocin are pituitary hormones.
So (3) is correct.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2018-q137",pn:137,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Reproductive Health",st:"Contraceptive Saheli",q:"The contraceptive 'SAHELI'",o:["blocks estrogen receptors in the uterus, preventing eggs from getting implanted.","increases the concentration of estrogen and prevents ovulation in females.","is an IUD","is a post-coital contraceptive."],c:0,s:`Saheli is a once-a-week non-steroidal oral pill developed in India.
It blocks estrogen receptors in the uterus, so implantation is prevented.
It is not an IUD and not a post-coital pill.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Reproductive Health"},{id:"neet-2018-q138",pn:138,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Spermiogenesis and spermiation",q:"The difference between spermiogenesis and spermiation is -",o:["In spermiogenesis spermatids are formed, while in spermiation spermatozoa are formed.","In spermiogenesis spermatozoa are formed, while in spermiation spermatids are formed.","In spermiogenesis spermatozoa from sertoli cells are released into the cavity of seminiferous tubules, while in spermiation spermatozoa are formed.","In spermiogensis spermatozoa are formed, while in spermiation spermatozoa are relaeased from sertoli cells into the cavity of seminiferous tubules."],c:3,s:`Spermiogenesis is the change of spermatids into spermatozoa (sperms).
Spermiation is the release of the sperms from Sertoli cells into the lumen of the seminiferous tubule.
So (4) is correct.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2018-q139",pn:139,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Foetal membranes",q:"The amnion of mammalian embryo is derived from -",o:["ectoderm and mesoderm","endoderm and mesoderm","mesoderm and trophoblast","ectoderm and endoderm"],c:0,s:"The amnion is formed from ectoderm and extra-embryonic mesoderm.",sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2018-q140",pn:140,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Age pyramids",q:"In a growing population of a country,",o:["pre-reproductive individuals are more than the reproductive individuals.","reproductive individuals are less than the post-reproductive individuals.","reproductive and pre-reproductive individuals are equal in number.","pre-reproductive individuals are less than the reproductive individuals."],c:0,s:`A growing (expanding) population has a pyramid with a broad base.
So pre-reproductive individuals are more than reproductive ones.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Organisms and Populations"},{id:"neet-2018-q141",pn:141,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"In-situ and ex-situ conservation",q:"All of the following are included in 'Ex-situ conservation' except -",o:["Wildlife safari parks","sacred groves","Botanical gardens","seed banks"],c:1,s:`Ex-situ conservation is outside the natural habitat: zoos, safari parks, botanical gardens and seed banks.
Sacred groves protect species in their natural habitat, so they are in-situ.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biodiversity and Conservation"},{id:"neet-2018-q142",pn:142,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Drugs from plants",q:"Which part of poppy plant is used to obtain the drug 'Smack' ?",o:["Flowers","Latex","Roots","Leaves"],c:1,s:`Morphine comes from the latex of the opium poppy (Papaver somniferum).
Smack (heroin) is made by acetylation of morphine, so latex is the source.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2018-q143",pn:143,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Environmental Chemistry",st:"Environmental terms",q:"Match the items given in Column-I with those in column-II. Column-I: a. Eutrophication, b. Sanitary landfill, c. Snow blindness, d. Jhum cultivation. Column-II: i. UV-B radiation, ii. Deforestation, iii. Nutrient enrichment, iv. Waste disposal.",o:["a-ii, b-i, c-iii, d-iv","a-i, b-iii, c-iv, d-ii","a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-i, b-ii, c-iv, d-iii"],c:2,s:`Eutrophication is nutrient enrichment of a water body: a\u2013iii.
Sanitary landfill is a method of waste disposal: b\u2013iv.
Snow blindness is caused by UV-B: c\u2013i.
Jhum cultivation (shifting cultivation) involves clearing forest: d\u2013ii.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Environmental Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2018-q144",pn:144,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population interactions",q:"Which one of the following population interactions is widely used in medical science for the production of antibiotics ?",o:["Commensalism","Mutualism","Parasitism","Amensalism"],c:3,s:`Antibiotics are chemicals made by one microbe that harm another microbe while the producer is unaffected.
This is antibiosis, a form of amensalism.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Organisms and Populations"},{id:"neet-2018-q145",pn:145,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Endoplasmic reticulum",q:"Which of the following events does not occur in rough endoplasmic reticulum ?",o:["protein folding","protein glycosylation","Cleavage of signal peptide","Phospholipid synthesis"],c:3,s:`Rough ER has ribosomes and handles protein folding, glycosylation and signal peptide cleavage.
Lipid and phospholipid synthesis takes place in smooth ER.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2018-q146",pn:146,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Mitochondrial sites of respiration",q:"Which of these statements is incorrect ?",o:["Enzymes of TCA cycle are present in mitochondrial matrix.","Glycolysis occurs in cytosol.","Glycolysis operates as long as it is supplied with NAD that can pick up hydrogen atoms.","Oxidative phosphorylation takes place in outer mitochondrial membrane."],c:3,s:`The TCA enzymes are in the matrix and glycolysis is in the cytosol, needing NAD\u207A to take up hydrogen.
Oxidative phosphorylation happens in the inner mitochondrial membrane (ETC and ATP synthase).
So (4) is incorrect.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Respiration in Plants"},{id:"neet-2018-q147",pn:147,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Translation",q:"Many ribosomes may associate with a single mRNA to form multiple copies of a polypeptide simultaneously. Such strings of ribosomes are termed as -",o:["Polysome","Polyhedral bodies","Plastidome","Nucleosome"],c:0,s:`Several ribosomes reading one mRNA form a string called a polysome (polyribosome).
This lets many copies of the polypeptide be made at once.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2018-q148",pn:148,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Types of chromosomes",q:"Select the incorrect match -",o:["Lampbrush chromosomes \u2013 Diplotene bivalents","Allosomes \u2013 Sex chromosomes","Submetacentric chromosomes \u2013 L-shaped chromosomes","Polytene chromosomes \u2013 Oocytes of amphibians"],c:3,s:`Lampbrush chromosomes are diplotene bivalents seen in amphibian oocytes.
Allosomes are sex chromosomes, and submetacentric chromosomes look L-shaped at anaphase.
Polytene chromosomes occur in salivary glands of Drosophila larvae, not in amphibian oocytes, so (4) is incorrect.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2018-q149",pn:149,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Neural Control and Coordination",st:"Nissl granules",q:"Nissl bodies are mainly composed of -",o:["Proteins and lipids","DNA and RNA","Nucleic acids and SER","Free ribosomes and RER"],c:3,s:`Nissl granules are in the cell body of neurons.
They are clusters of free ribosomes and rough ER, and they make proteins.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Neural Control and Coordination"},{id:"neet-2018-q150",pn:150,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Human dentition",q:"Which of the following terms describe human dentition ?",o:["Thecodont, Diphyodont, Homodont.","Thecodont, Diphyodont, Heterodont.","Pleurodont, Monophyodont, Homodont.","Pleurodont, Diphyodont, Heterodont."],c:1,s:`Human teeth are set in sockets in the jaw bone: thecodont.
Two sets appear in life, milk teeth and permanent teeth: diphyodont.
There are incisors, canines, premolars and molars: heterodont.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Digestion and Absorption"},{id:"neet-2018-q151",pn:151,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Excretory Products and their Elimination",st:"Disorders of the excretory system",q:"Match the items given in Column I with those in Column II and select the correct option given below : Column I: a. Glycosuria, b. Gout, c. Renal calculi, d. Glomerular nephritis. Column II: i. Accumulation of uric acid in joints, ii. Mass of crystallized salts within the kidney, iii. Inflammation in glomeruli, iv. Presence of glucose in urine",o:["a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i","a-i, b-ii, c-iii, d-iv","a-ii, b-iii, c-i, d-iv","a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii"],c:3,s:`Glycosuria means glucose in urine: a\u2013iv.
Gout is uric acid deposits in joints: b\u2013i.
Renal calculi are stones of crystallised salts in the kidney: c\u2013ii.
Glomerular nephritis is inflammation of the glomeruli: d\u2013iii.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Excretory Products and their Elimination"},{id:"neet-2018-q152",pn:152,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Excretory Products and their Elimination",st:"Parts of the excretory system",q:"Match the items given in column I with those in Column II. Column I (Function): a. Ultrafiltration, b. Concentration of urine, c. Transport of urine, d. Storage of urine. Column II (Part of Excretory System): i. Henle's loop, ii. Ureter, iii. Urinary bladder, iv. Malpighian corpuscle, v. Proximal convoluted tubule",o:["a-iv, b-v, c-ii, d-iii","a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii","a-v, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-v, b-iv, c-i, d-iii"],c:1,s:`Ultrafiltration happens in the Malpighian corpuscle (glomerulus plus Bowman's capsule): a\u2013iv.
Henle's loop helps concentrate urine: b\u2013i.
Ureters carry urine to the bladder: c\u2013ii.
The urinary bladder stores urine: d\u2013iii.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Excretory Products and their Elimination"},{id:"neet-2018-q153",pn:153,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"Homologous organs",q:"The similarity of bone structure in the forelimbs of many vertebrates is an example of",o:["Homology","Analogy","Convergent evolution","Adaptive radiation"],c:0,s:`Organs with the same basic structure and origin but different functions are homologous.
Vertebrate forelimbs (whale flipper, bat wing, human arm) have the same bone plan.
So this is homology.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2018-q154",pn:154,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Autoimmune diseases",q:"Which of the following is not an autoimmune disease?",o:["Psoriasis","Rheumatoid arthritis","Alzheimer's disease","Vitiligo"],c:2,s:`In autoimmune diseases the body's immune system attacks its own cells.
Psoriasis, rheumatoid arthritis and vitiligo are autoimmune.
Alzheimer's disease is a degeneration of neurons and is not autoimmune.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2018-q155",pn:155,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"Divergent and convergent evolution",q:"Among the following sets of examples for divergent evolution, select the incorrect option :",o:["Forelimbs of man, bat and cheetah","Heart of bat, man and cheetah","Brain of bat, man and cheetah","Eye of octopus, bat and man"],c:3,s:`Divergent evolution produces homologous structures from a common ancestral origin.
Forelimbs, hearts and brains of mammals are homologous.
The eye of octopus and the eyes of mammals have different origins and are analogous (convergent evolution), so (4) is incorrect.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2018-q156",pn:156,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Inheritance of ABO blood groups",q:"Which of the following characteristics represent 'Inheritance of blood groups' in humans? a. Dominance b. Co-dominance c. Multiple allele d. Incomplete dominance e. Polygenic inheritance",o:["b, c and e","a, b and c","b, d and e","a, c and e"],c:1,s:`ABO blood group is controlled by gene I with three alleles (I\u1D2C, I\u1D2E, i): multiple alleles.
I\u1D2C and I\u1D2E are each dominant over i: dominance.
I\u1D2C and I\u1D2E together both express: co-dominance.
It is not incomplete dominance or polygenic.
So a, b and c.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2018-q157",pn:157,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Elephantiasis",q:"In which disease does mosquito transmitted pathogen cause chronic inflammation of lymphatic vessels?",o:["Elephantiasis","Ascariasis","Ringworm disease","Amoebiasis"],c:0,s:`Elephantiasis (filariasis) is caused by the filarial worm Wuchereria, spread by mosquitoes.
The worms live in the lymphatic vessels and cause chronic inflammation there.
Ascariasis, ringworm and amoebiasis are not spread by mosquitoes.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2018-q158",pn:158,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Microbes in Human Welfare",st:"Curd",q:"Conversion of milk to curd improves its nutritional value by increasing the amount of",o:["Vitamin D","Vitamin A","Vitamin B\u2081\u2082","Vitamin E"],c:2,s:`Lactic acid bacteria (LAB) set milk into curd.
They also increase vitamin B\u2081\u2082 in the curd, which improves its nutritional value.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Microbes in Human Welfare"},{id:"neet-2018-q159",pn:159,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Hormone chemistry",q:"Which of the following is an amino acid derived hormone?",o:["Epinephrine","Ecdysone","Estradiol","Estriol"],c:0,s:`Epinephrine (adrenaline) is made from the amino acid tyrosine.
Ecdysone, estradiol and estriol are steroid hormones.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Chemical Coordination and Integration"},{id:"neet-2018-q160",pn:160,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Neural Control and Coordination",st:"Brain regions",q:"Which of the following structures or regions is incorrectly paired with its function?",o:["Medulla oblongata \u2013 Controls respiration and cardiovascular reflexes","Limbic system \u2013 Consists of fibre tracts that interconnect different regions of brain; controls movement.","Hypothalamus \u2013 Production of releasing hormones and regulation of temperature, hunger and thirst.","Corpus callosum \u2013 Band of fibers connecting left and right cerebral hemispheres."],c:1,s:`(1), (3) and (4) are correct.
The limbic system is the inner part of the cerebrum that works with the hypothalamus in emotions, memory and behaviour like sexual response.
It does not control movement, so (2) is wrongly paired.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Neural Control and Coordination"},{id:"neet-2018-q161",pn:161,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Hormones and bone",q:"Which of the following hormones can play a significant role in osteoporosis?",o:["Aldosterone and Prolactin","Progesterone and Aldosterone","Estrogen and Parathyroid hormone","Parathyroid hormone and Prolactin"],c:2,s:`Estrogen lack after menopause increases bone loss, a main cause of osteoporosis.
Parathyroid hormone controls calcium release from bone.
So estrogen and parathyroid hormone are significant.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Chemical Coordination and Integration"},{id:"neet-2018-q162",pn:162,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Neural Control and Coordination",st:"Eye",q:"The transparent lens in the human eye is held in its place by",o:["ligaments attached to the ciliary body","ligaments attached to the iris","smooth muscles attached to the iris","smooth muscles attached to the ciliary body"],c:0,s:"The lens is held by suspensory ligaments that are attached to the ciliary body.",sub:"Zoology",ch:"Neural Control and Coordination"},{id:"neet-2018-q163",pn:163,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Metamorphosis",q:"Which of the following animals does not undergo metamorphosis?",o:["Earthworm","Tunicate","Moth","Starfish"],c:0,s:`Earthworm has direct development.
Tunicate (larva), moth (larva and pupa) and starfish (bipinnaria larva) show metamorphosis.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2018-q164",pn:164,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Birds",q:"Identify the vertebrate group of animals characterized by crop and gizzard in its digestive system.",o:["Amphibia","Reptilia","Aves","Osteichthyes"],c:2,s:"Birds (Aves) have a crop for storing food and a gizzard for grinding it.",sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2018-q165",pn:165,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Diatoms",q:"Which of the following organisms are known as chief producers in the oceans?",o:["Dinoflagellates","Diatoms","Cyanobacteria","Euglenoids"],c:1,s:`Diatoms are the main photosynthetic organisms (phytoplankton) in the oceans.
They are called chief producers of the oceans.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2018-q166",pn:166,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Homeotherms",q:"Which one of these animals is not a homeotherm?",o:["Macropus","Chelone","Camelus","Psittacula"],c:1,s:`Homeotherms keep a constant body temperature: Macropus (kangaroo, mammal), Camelus (camel, mammal) and Psittacula (parrot, bird).
Chelone is a turtle, a reptile, which is poikilothermic.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2018-q167",pn:167,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Ciliated protozoans",q:"Ciliates differ from all other protozoan in",o:["using flagella for locomotion","having a contractile vacuole for removing excess water","using pseudopodia for capturing prey","having two types of nuclei"],c:3,s:`Ciliates such as Paramecium have a macronucleus and a micronucleus.
Flagella and pseudopodia are used by other protozoans, and contractile vacuoles occur in other protozoans too.
So two types of nuclei is the difference.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2018-q168",pn:168,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Cockroach",q:"Which of the following features is used to identify a male cockroach from a female cockroach?",o:["Presence of a boat shaped sternum on the 9th abdominal segment","Presence of caudal styles","Forewings with darker tegmina","presence of anal cerci"],c:1,s:`Males have a pair of thread-like anal styles on the 9th segment; females do not.
Both sexes have anal cerci. The boat-shaped sternum is a female feature.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Structural Organisation in Animals"},{id:"neet-2018-q169",pn:169,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Respiratory disorders",q:"Which of the following options correctly represents the lung conditions in asthma and emphysema, respectively?",o:["Inflammation of bronchioles; Decreased respiratory surface","Increased number of bronchioles; Increased respiratory surface","Increased respiratory surface; Inflammation of bronchioles","Decreased respiratory surface; Inflammation of bronchioles"],c:0,s:`Asthma is inflammation of bronchi and bronchioles that gives difficulty in breathing.
Emphysema damages the alveolar walls, so the respiratory surface decreases.
So asthma then emphysema is inflammation of bronchioles, then decreased respiratory surface.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases"},{id:"neet-2018-q170",pn:170,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Heart valves",q:"Match the items given in Column I with those in Column II. Column I: a. Tricuspid valve, b. Bicuspid valve, c. Semilunar valve. Column II: i. Between left atrium and left ventricle, ii. Between right ventricle and pulmonary artery, iii. Between right atrium and right ventricle",o:["a-iii, b-i, c-ii","a-i, b-iii, c-ii","a-i, b-ii, c-iii","a-ii, b-i, c-iii"],c:0,s:`The tricuspid valve guards the right atrium and right ventricle opening: a\u2013iii.
The bicuspid (mitral) valve is between left atrium and left ventricle: b\u2013i.
Semilunar valves are at the base of the pulmonary artery and aorta: c\u2013ii.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Body Fluids and Circulation"},{id:"neet-2018-q171",pn:171,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Respiratory volumes",q:"Match the items given in Column I with those in Column II. Column I: a. Tidal volume, b. Inspiratory Reserve volume, c. Expiratory Reserve volume, d. Residual volume. Column II: i. 2500 \u2013 3000 mL, ii. 1100 \u2013 1200 mL, iii. 500 \u2013 550 mL, iv. 1000 \u2013 1100 mL",o:["a-iii, b-ii, c-i, d-iv","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-i, b-iv, c-ii, d-iii","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i"],c:1,s:`Tidal volume is about 500 mL: a\u2013iii (500\u2013550 mL).
Inspiratory reserve volume is 2500\u20133000 mL: b\u2013i.
Expiratory reserve volume is 1000\u20131100 mL: c\u2013iv.
Residual volume is 1100\u20131200 mL: d\u2013ii.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases"},{id:"neet-2018-q172",pn:172,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Transcription",q:"AGGTATCGCAT is a sequence from the coding strand of a gene. What will be the corresponding sequence of the transcribed mRNA?",o:["AGGUAUCGCAU","UGGTUTCGCAT","ACCUAUGCGAU","UCCAUAGCGUA"],c:0,s:`The mRNA is made on the template strand, so it has the same sequence as the coding strand but with U in place of T.
AGGTATCGCAT becomes AGGUAUCGCAU.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2018-q173",pn:173,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"Hugo de Vries",q:"According to Hugo de Vries, the mechanism of evolution is",o:["Multiple step mutations","Saltation","Phenotypic variations","Minor mutations"],c:1,s:"De Vries worked on evening primrose and proposed that evolution occurs by large, single-step mutations called saltations.",sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2018-q174",pn:174,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Menstrual cycle phases",q:"Match the items given in Column I with those in Column II. Column I: a. Proliferative Phase, b. Secretory Phase, c. Menstruation. Column II: i. Breakdown of endometrial lining, ii. Follicular Phase, iii. Luteal Phase",o:["a-iii, b-ii, c-i","a-i, b-iii, c-ii","a-ii, b-iii, c-i","a-iii, b-i, c-ii"],c:2,s:`The proliferative phase of the uterus matches the ovarian follicular phase: a\u2013ii.
The secretory phase matches the luteal phase: b\u2013iii.
Menstruation is the breakdown of the endometrial lining: c\u2013i.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2018-q175",pn:175,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"X-linked inheritance",q:"A woman has an X-linked condition on one of her X chromosomes. This chromosome can be inherited by",o:["Only daughters","Only sons","Only grandchildren","Both sons and daughters"],c:3,s:`A woman passes one of her two X chromosomes to every child.
So the affected X can go to both sons and daughters.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2018-q176",pn:176,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Operon",q:"All of the following are part of an operon except",o:["an operator","structural genes","an enhancer","a promoter"],c:2,s:`An operon has structural genes, a promoter and an operator, controlled by a regulator gene.
An enhancer is a feature of eukaryotic gene regulation, not part of an operon.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2018-q177",pn:177,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Gastric cells",q:"Which of the following gastric cells indirectly help in erythropoiesis?",o:["Chief cells","Mucous cells","Goblet cells","Parietal cells"],c:3,s:`Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete HCl and the intrinsic factor.
Intrinsic factor is needed to absorb vitamin B\u2081\u2082, which red blood cell formation needs.
So parietal cells help erythropoiesis indirectly.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Digestion and Absorption"},{id:"neet-2018-q178",pn:178,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Plasma proteins",q:"Match the items given in Column I with those in Column II. Column I: a. Fibrinogen, b. Globulin, c. Albumin. Column II: i. Osmotic balance, ii. Blood clotting, iii. Defence mechanism",o:["a-iii, b-ii, c-i","a-i, b-ii, c-iii","a-i, b-iii, c-ii","a-ii, b-iii, c-i"],c:3,s:`Fibrinogen takes part in blood clotting: a\u2013ii.
Globulins (including antibodies) are for defence: b\u2013iii.
Albumin maintains osmotic balance: c\u2013i.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Body Fluids and Circulation"},{id:"neet-2018-q179",pn:179,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Muscle contraction",q:"Calcium is important in skeletal muscle contraction because it",o:["binds to troponin to remove the masking of active sites on actin for myosin","activates the myosin ATPase by binding to it.","detaches the myosin head from the actin filament.","prevents the formation of bonds between the myosin cross bridges and the actin filament."],c:0,s:`On a nerve signal, Ca\xB2\u207A is released into the sarcoplasm.
Ca\xB2\u207A binds to troponin, and the troponin\u2013tropomyosin complex moves away from the active sites on actin.
Myosin heads can then bind actin and form cross bridges.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Locomotion and Movement"},{id:"neet-2018-q180",pn:180,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Occupational lung disease",q:"Which of the following is an occupational respiratory disorder?",o:["Anthracis","Silicosis","Botulism","Emphysema"],c:1,s:`Silicosis is caused by inhaling silica dust in stone grinding and quarrying, so it is occupational.
Anthrax and botulism are bacterial diseases, and emphysema is mainly caused by smoking.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases"}]})();
